# errors-resolution.topic -- errors of names, types, structs, enums,
# methods, flags, refinements, imports and modules; rule.type-* of TYPE.

@topic error.unbound
title: UNBOUND: a name used where no binding of it is visible
summary: A name does not resolve to any binding at the point where it is used: it is misspelled, never defined, defined further down, or names a type or an enum case rather than a value.
index: errors
alias: unbound name; undefined name; name is not defined
keywords: unbound, undefined, not defined, declared later, forward reference, binding, scope, typo, hoisting
related: concept.bindings concept.definition-order rule.forward-references rule.no-callable-types concept.enums concept.mutual-recursion concept.imports error.missing-import
docs: STRICT-REFERENCE-DETERMINISM.md README.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND

== MEANING
Every name a program uses is resolved at compile time to exactly one
binding: a value binding (`x = ...`), a function, a parameter, a loop
variable, a root primitive (`true`, `mod`, `argv`), or -- written
qualified -- a member of a namespace (`std::list::at`). A binding is visible
from the point where it is established to the end of its scope. Nothing
is hoisted, and there is no lookup by name at run time. UNBOUND reports a
reference that resolves to nothing.

The message says which case it is:

    unbound name "count"
        no binding of that name is visible here at all;
    unbound name "later": "later" is declared later; forward references
    are not allowed (a binding is visible only after it is established)
        there is one, but further down: a forward reference;
    unbound name "Color": "Color" is an enum type, not a value; ...
    unbound name "Red": an enum case is never visible by its bare name;
    write it qualified by its enum's type, e.g. Color::Red
    unbound name "m::f": "f" is not established before this point in its
    own namespace; ...
        (inside module m, a qualified forward reference).

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A typo, or a name from another language (`len`, `print`, `string`).
  * A function called above the line that defines it. Functions are
    bindings like any other: a function may call itself and what is
    defined above it, never what is defined below it -- also when the
    call only runs later. Write callees first.
  * Two functions that call each other. One of the two calls is always a
    forward reference, so plain mutual recursion is rejected; `mutual:`
    groups exist only as a design (MUTUAL-RECURSION.md), not in this
    compiler.
  * A bare enum case (`Red`) or the enum type itself used as a value.
  * A type used as a function or predicate: `Small(3)`, `Small?(x)`, or a
    struct name `P` alone. No type is callable (rule.no-callable-types).
  * A namespace member used without its qualifier: after `import std::list`
    the function is `std::list::length(xs)` (or `xs.length()`), never a bare
    `length(xs)`; an import never adds unqualified names.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Read the quoted name and the clause after the colon. Without a clause,
search the file for a definition: none means a typo or a missing
definition; one in a module means the use must be written qualified
(`ns::name`, after `import ns`). With "declared later", find the
definition and move it above the first use, together with what it needs.

== WHAT TO DO
  * Define every function before the first function that calls it.
  * For two functions that need each other, pass one to the other as an
    argument: functions that reach each other through arguments are
    legal (second example below).
  * Write enum cases qualified: `Color::Red`.
  * Instead of `T(x)` or `T?(x)`, call a function you write: a converter
    whose declared result is the type, or a predicate with a `proves`
    clause (concept.refinements, concept.integer-domains).

The compiled program never reads an unresolved name: the interpreter's
own run-time check ("name ... used before its binding") guards core IR
text only, never a source program the front end accepted.

@example reject
total = count + 1
total
@caption A name that is defined nowhere
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND
@at 1:9
@end

@example accept
fn is_even_step(n: int, odd) -> bool:
    if n == 0:
        return true
    odd(n - 1)

fn is_odd(n: int) -> bool:
    if n == 0:
        return false
    is_even_step(n - 1, is_odd)

is_odd(7)
@caption Mutual recursion through an argument
@value true
@end

@topic error.duplicate
title: DUPLICATE: a name, error or handler given twice where one is allowed
summary: Two bindings of one name in the same scope, the same error twice in an errors clause, two handlers for one error on one call, or two extern C declarations of one name.
index: errors
alias: already bound; rebinding a name
keywords: duplicate, rebinding, reassignment, same scope, handler, errors clause, parameter, shadow
related: concept.bindings rule.immutable-bindings capability.assignment rule.one-handler-per-error concept.errors concept.c-ffi error.unbound error.duplicate-field
docs: README.md STRICT-REFERENCE-DETERMINISM.md C-FFI.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE

== MEANING
Botlish bindings are immutable and a name is bound once per scope:
`x = 1` followed by `x = 2` in the same scope is not an update, there is
no assignment at all, and a second binding would make `x` mean two
things. The same one-name-one-meaning rule covers the other lists that
name things:

    duplicate binding "x" in the same lexical scope
    duplicate binding "a" in the same lexical scope (block parameters)
    duplicate error "Boom" in the same function's "errors" declaration
    duplicate "on Boom" handler in the same handled call (error "Boom"
    is already handled)
    extern c fn "abs" is already declared in the entry program (at ...):
    two declarations of one Botlish name are never merged, whatever their
    signatures
    duplicate parameter "x" of extern c fn "f"

A nested scope (a function body, an `if` arm, a loop body) may bind a
name its enclosing scope also binds. That is a new binding, visible only
inside the nested scope; the outer binding is unchanged after it.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Updating a variable as in an imperative language: `total = total + x`
    in the same scope, or a counter "incremented" in place.
  * Two parameters of one function with the same name.
  * The same error listed twice after `errors`, or two `on E:` handlers
    for the same E on one call (often after a copy and paste).
  * Two `extern c fn` declarations of one name (C has no overloading in
    Botlish either).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The location is the second binding; search the scope for the first one.
For a binding, ask what the second one was meant to change: Botlish
computes a new value under a new name instead of changing an old one.

== WHAT TO DO
  * Give the new value its own name (`subtotal`, `total`), or compute it
    in one expression: an `if` expression or a collecting loop is a value
    (concept.if-expressions, concept.loops).
  * Accumulate with a loop that produces the list of values, or with a
    recursive helper function that takes the running value as a
    parameter.
  * Merge duplicate handlers into one `on E:` body; list each error once.

@example reject
x = 1
x = 2
x
@caption A second binding of x in the same scope
@error CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE
@at 2:1
@end

@example accept
x = 1
if true:
    x = 2
x
@caption A nested scope's binding is a new one
@value 1
@end

@topic error.arity
title: ARITY: a call with the wrong number of arguments
summary: A call passes a different number of arguments than its callee takes: some calls are rejected at compile time; an ordinary call with the wrong count is only stopped when it runs.
index: errors
alias: wrong number of arguments; argument count; too many arguments; too few arguments
keywords: arity, arguments, parameters, count, flags, layout_length, extern
related: concept.functions concept.flags rule.flags concept.binary-layouts error.unknown-flag error.type
docs: FLAGS.md BINARY-LAYOUT.md README.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC ARITY

== MEANING
A call must pass exactly as many arguments as its callee has parameters
(flags are not counted: they are written as `:name`), or, to a variadic
function (concept.variadics), at least its fixed ones. A Botlish function
has no default arguments or optional parameters.

What is checked when:

  * At compile time, reported as this code:
        function emit takes 1 ordinary argument(s) and the flags :quiet,
        got 2 ordinary argument(s)
            a call of a function that declares flags (FLAGS.md);
        std::binary::layout_length takes exactly one argument, a layout name
        (2 given)
            the layout queries;
        a C function of N parameter(s) ... passes M argument(s)
            a call of an `extern c fn`, and the raw syscall transports.
  * At run time, as the same code, for every other call. Today the
    compiler does not reject a call of an ordinary function or of an
    intrinsic with the wrong number of arguments: the program compiles,
    and the call stops the program when (and only if) it runs:
        block (a b) expects 2 argument(s), got 1
        std::list::length expects 1 argument(s), got 2

A call through a value of a structural function type (`Fn{args: [...]}`)
with the wrong count is a TYPE error instead ("this call passes 2
argument(s) to a callable of function type ..."); resuming a coroutine
with the wrong count is COROUTINE-RESUME-ARITY.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A function's parameter list changed and a call was not updated.
  * A flag written as an ordinary argument (`emit(x, true)` for
    `emit(x, :quiet)`), or an argument forgotten before the flags.
  * An argument that was meant as the receiver of method syntax written
    twice: `xs.length(xs)` is `std::list::length(xs, xs)`.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Compare the call with the callee's declaration (`-help NAME` shows the
signature of a standard-library function). A run-time ARITY prints no
source location: the message names the callee's parameters
(`block (a b)`) or the intrinsic; search for the calls of that function.

== WHAT TO DO
Pass exactly one argument per parameter, flags as `:name`. Declaring
parameter types does not add a compile-time count check today, so keep
calls and declarations in step; tests that run every call catch a
mismatch.

@example reject
fn emit(x, flags :quiet):
    x

emit(1, 2, :quiet)
@caption Ordinary arguments of a function with flags
@error CORE SEMANTIC ARITY
@at 4:1
@end

@example reject
import std::binary

layout Header:
    size 2
    align 1
    field tag: std::binary::U8 at 0
    field len: std::binary::U8 at 1

std::binary::layout_length(Header, 1)
@caption A layout query takes exactly the layout
@error CORE SEMANTIC ARITY
@at 9:1
@end

@topic error.duplicate-loop-variable
title: DUPLICATE-LOOP-VARIABLE: one name bound by two clauses of a lockstep loop
summary: Two iteration clauses of one lockstep loop bind the same variable name; each clause needs its own name.
index: errors
alias: lockstep variable twice
keywords: lockstep, loop, iteration clause, variable, and
related: concept.lockstep concept.loops error.duplicate
docs: COLLECTING-LOOPS.md README.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE-LOOP-VARIABLE

== MEANING
A lockstep loop advances several iteration clauses together
(`loop x in xs and i from 0 to xs.length():`), binding one variable per
clause in the loop body. Two clauses binding the same name would leave
the body unable to say which value it means, so the loop is rejected:

    lockstep loop variable "x" is bound by more than one iteration clause

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A copied clause whose variable was not renamed.
  * Two lists walked together under one name (`loop x in a and x in b:`).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The location is the loop; the message names the variable. Read the
variable before `in` (or `from`) in each `and` clause.

== WHAT TO DO
Give each clause its own variable: `loop a_item in a and b_item in b:`.

@example reject
loop x in [1, 2] and x in [3, 4]:
    x
@caption Both clauses bind x
@error CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE-LOOP-VARIABLE
@at 1:1
@end

@example accept
pairs = loop x in [1, 2] and y in [3, 4]:
    x * y
pairs
@caption One name per clause
@value [3, 8]
@end

@topic error.type
title: TYPE: a value, annotation or declaration that does not fit its type
summary: The broad type diagnostic: a value cannot be proven to have the type its context declares, a type expression or declaration is invalid, or, at run time, an intrinsic got an argument of the wrong kind.
index: errors
alias: type error; type mismatch; wrong type
keywords: type, cannot be proven, declared type, admissible, annotation, parameter type, result type, expected, got, kind
related: rule.type-argument rule.type-result rule.type-field rule.type-annotation rule.type-generic-call rule.type-declaration concept.types proof.types rule.typed-parameters concept.function-types concept.mutable-vector concept.integer-domains
docs: STRICT-TYPED-PARAMETERS.md MINIMAL-APPLIED-LIST-TYPES.md STRUCTURAL-FUNCTION-TYPES.md TYPED-CALLABLE-ESCAPE-SOUNDNESS.md README.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
code: SURFACE MODULE TYPE

== MEANING
Botlish's declared types are compile-time proof obligations, not
run-time checks. Where a program declares a type -- a parameter
(`x: Digit`), a result (`-> str`), a struct field, an error's payload
field, a MutableVector's element type, a structural function type -- the
compiler must *prove* that every value reaching that place has the type.
A value that is merely not proven is rejected exactly like one that is
proven wrong: there is no third outcome that inserts a run-time guard,
and nothing re-checks a declared type when the program runs. So the body
of `fn show(d: Digit)` may rely on 0 <= d <= 9 with no check of its own.

TYPE is the code of every such failure, and of invalid type expressions
and declarations. The most common families have pages of their own (the
help line names the right one; SPECIFIC RULES below lists them):

    argument for parameter "x" cannot be proven to satisfy T
        rule.type-argument
    function result does not prove declared type T
        rule.type-result
    field "f" of struct S cannot be proven to satisfy its declared type T
        rule.type-field
    unknown or invalid type X for parameter "x"
        rule.type-annotation
    call to f with x : T is invalid: ...  /  ..., the parameter type
    inferred from the function body
        rule.type-generic-call
    type "P" is already declared  /  duplicate field "x" in struct "P"
        rule.type-declaration

Other compile-time families, reported as TYPE with this page:

  * Calls through a structural function type: "argument 1 cannot be
    proven to satisfy int, the parameter type the callee's function type
    Fn{...} requires", "this call passes 2 argument(s) to a callable of
    function type ..., whose contract takes 1".
  * Mutable containers: "value of type str is not admissible to element
    type int of MutableVector[int]": an element type is fixed for the
    container's lifetime, and a store never widens it.
  * Handlers: "handler "on E" completes with type str, not admissible as
    int (the call's own normal result type)": a handled call is one
    value, so each handler must produce the call's type.
  * Contracts of function values: a typed function passed where its
    parameter types or declared errors would be forgotten ("has typed
    parameter requirements that cannot be preserved ...", "a value of
    function type ... is used here as a value of function type ...,
    which does not declare it").
  * The C ABI: an `extern c fn` signature that is not one ("unit is not a C
    parameter type"), and `std::ffi::c::Void {...}` (SURFACE MODULE TYPE,
    reported by the loader: C's void has no values).

At run time, TYPE has a different source. The primitive operations and
the standard-library intrinsics check the kinds of their arguments when
they run, and a value of the wrong kind stops the program with this code:

    +: expected int, got "a"
    std::str::length: expected str, got 5

A direct call of an intrinsic or operator is not rejected at compile
time for an argument of the wrong kind (`1 + "a"` compiles); the
argument is rejected statically only when it flows into a declared or
inferred parameter type of a Botlish function.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A value whose type the compiler does not know precisely enough:
    an untyped parameter (its type is `any`), a value read from a list of
    mixed values, an `int` where an integer domain (`Digit`) is declared.
  * A real mismatch: a str where an int is declared, a List[int] where a
    List[str] is declared.
  * A type name that does not exist (`Int`, `String`, `float`).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Read the declared type and the "(argument type: ..., facts: ...)" part:
the argument type is what the compiler knows; the facts are the integer
range it proved. `facts: [-∞, +∞]` with an integer domain declared means
nothing bounded the value. Then follow the help line to the specific
rule page, which says how to make such a value provable.

== WHAT TO DO
Make the value provably of the type -- declare the type where the value
comes from (a parameter or a function result), guard an integer with
comparisons before the call, convert through a function whose declared
result is the type -- or change the declaration to what the value is.
For a run-time TYPE, the message names the intrinsic and the value: find
where that value came from and convert it before the call.

@example reject
import std::mutable_vector

fn add_name(v: MutableVector[int]) -> int:
    v.push("seven")
    0

0
@caption A str pushed onto a MutableVector[int]
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 4:12
@end

@example reject
import std::ffi::c

std::ffi::c::Void {}
@caption C's void has no values
@error SURFACE MODULE TYPE
@at 3:1
@end

@topic rule.type-generic-call
title: Calls of untyped functions are checked against what their bodies need
summary: An untyped (generic) parameter is checked at each call against what the function's body does with it: an argument the body cannot accept rejects the call.
index: rules errors
alias: generic function call invalid; inferred parameter type
keywords: untyped parameter, generic, inferred, instance, specialization, call to, is invalid, statically incompatible, sound type
related: error.type concept.specialization concept.functions rule.type-argument rule.typed-parameters error.unproven-field
docs: STRICT-TYPED-PARAMETERS.md INTRINSIC-FUNCTION-CONTRACT-INFERENCE.md OPPORTUNISTIC-SEMANTIC-INSTANCES.md
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | call to * is invalid: *
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | *the parameter type inferred from the function body*
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | cannot infer a sound type for parameter *

== THE RULE
A parameter without a type annotation is not unchecked. The compiler
checks every call of the function in two ways:

  * Inferred contract. From the body, it infers what each untyped
    parameter must be: `x + 1` needs an int, `if x:` a bool, a call
    `g(x)` whatever g's parameter needs. An argument statically of
    another type is rejected at the call ("argument for parameter "x" is
    statically incompatible with int, the parameter type inferred from
    the function body"), with the reason ("inferred from the function
    body: argument 1 of native + requires int (at 2:5)"). If two uses in
    the body need types that nothing satisfies, the function itself is
    rejected ("cannot infer a sound type for parameter ...").
  * Semantic instance. A direct call is also analyzed with the actual
    argument types, as if the body had been written for them. A problem
    found there is reported at the call that made it:
        call to first with x : int is invalid: cannot project field
        "value" from a value of type int: only a struct has fields
        (in the body of first, at 2:5)

== WHY
Untyped functions are Botlish's generic functions: one definition serves
every argument type its body can handle, with no type parameters. The
price is that a problem shows up where a call supplies an argument the
body cannot handle -- so that is where it is reported, with the place in
the body that cannot handle it.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
An inferred contract is reported at the call's argument, a semantic
instance's problem at the call, and "cannot infer a sound type" at the
use in the body that conflicts. The part after "is invalid:" or
"inferred from the function body:" is the problem inside the function,
with its location there.

== WHAT TO DO
  * Pass an argument the body can use, or convert it before the call.
  * Declare the parameter's type (`fn inc(x: int) -> int`): the error
    then moves to the call as a plain parameter-type error, and the body
    is checked once against the declaration.
  * For "cannot infer a sound type", the body uses one parameter in two
    incompatible ways (as a number and as a condition): split the
    function or fix the use that is wrong.

@example reject
fn inc(x):
    x + 1

inc("one")
@caption The body adds 1 to x, so x must be an int
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 4:5
@end

@example reject
fn first(x):
    x.value

first(3)
@caption The instance for x : int reads a field
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 4:1
@end

@example reject
fn g(x):
    x + 1

fn h(x):
    g(x)
    if x:
        1
    else:
        2

h(1)
@caption x is used as an int and as a condition
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 5:7
@end

@example accept
struct Box:
    value: int

fn first(x):
    x.value

first(Box {value: 3})
@caption An argument the body can use
@value 3
@end

@topic rule.type-argument
title: An argument must be proven to satisfy its declared parameter type
summary: At every call whose callee is known, each argument for a typed parameter must be proven to have that type at compile time; nothing checks it at run time.
index: rules errors
alias: parameter type mismatch
keywords: argument, parameter, typed parameter, cannot be proven to satisfy, facts, integer domain, admissible, call
related: error.type rule.typed-parameters rule.type-result rule.type-generic-call concept.types concept.integer-domains proof.types proof.ranges
docs: STRICT-TYPED-PARAMETERS.md SOURCE-DEFINED-INTEGER-DOMAINS.md MINIMAL-APPLIED-LIST-TYPES.md
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | *argument for parameter * cannot be proven to satisfy*

== THE RULE
A parameter annotation (`fn show(d: Digit)`) is a compile-time proof
obligation at each call whose target is known: the compiler must prove
that every value the argument can have is a value of the declared type.
It proves it either by type (the argument's type is the declared type or
a subtype: a `Tiny` where its parent `Small` is declared) or, for integer
domains, by range (the argument's proven range lies inside the domain:
the literal 7 for `Int in 0..9`, or an int guarded by
`if n < 0 or n > 9: return ...`).

List element types must match exactly (List[T] is invariant today): a
List[Digit] is not admissible for List[int], and a literal `[1, 2]` is a
List[int], not a List[Digit], whatever its elements' values. Build such
a list from values that already have the element type
(`[digit(1), digit(2)]`, with `fn digit(n: Digit) -> Digit`).

== WHY
Types are proven, never checked at run time: no guard is inserted for a
typed parameter, so the function's body can rely on its declaration
(that d is between 0 and 9) without checking again, on every backend, at
no cost. The price is that the caller must establish it. An unproven
argument is rejected exactly like a wrong one, because there is no
run-time check to catch it later.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
    argument for parameter "d" cannot be proven to satisfy int[Digit]
    (argument type: int, facts: [-∞, +∞])
at the argument. The argument type is what the compiler knows of the
value; "facts" is its proven integer range ([12, 12] {12} for the
literal 12, [-∞, +∞] for an unconstrained int). `int[Digit]` is how an
integer domain prints.

== WHAT TO DO
  * Declare the type where the value comes from: give the caller's own
    parameter the same type (`fn relay(n: Digit)`), so its callers prove
    it, or call a function whose declared result is the type.
  * Guard an integer before the call, in a form the range analysis
    reads: `if n < 0 or n > 9: return 0` then `show(n)`, or
    `if n >= 0 and n <= 9: show(n)`.
  * For a real mismatch, convert the value or change the declaration.

@example reject
type Digit = Int in 0..9

fn show(d: Digit) -> int:
    d * 10

fn relay(n: int) -> int:
    show(n)

relay(3)
@caption Nothing bounds n to 0..9
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 7:10
@end

@example accept
type Digit = Int in 0..9

fn show(d: Digit) -> int:
    d * 10

fn show_or_zero(n: int) -> int:
    if n < 0 or n > 9:
        return 0
    show(n)

show_or_zero(7)
@caption A guard proves the range before the call
@value 70
@end

@topic rule.type-result
title: A function's result must prove its declared result type
summary: With a declared result type (-> T), every normal completion of the function -- its last value and every return -- must be proven to have type T.
index: rules errors
alias: function result does not prove declared type; result type mismatch
keywords: result type, return type, declared result, return, does not prove, ->
related: error.type rule.type-argument concept.functions concept.integer-domains proof.types principle.guard-clauses
docs: CROSS-MODULE-REFINED-SIGNATURES.md STRICT-TYPED-PARAMETERS.md
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | function result does not prove declared type *

== THE RULE
`fn f(...) -> T:` declares that every normal completion of f is a T: the
value of the body's last expression and the value of every `return`.
The compiler proves it from the body, by type or, for an integer domain,
by the proven range. A declared error (`fail E`) is not a normal
completion and needs no proof.

== WHY
The declared result is what every caller sees instead of what the body
happens to compute: callers rely on it without checks (it is a proof
obligation of the callee, never a run-time cast), and the body can
change without changing the callers' view. A converter into an integer
domain is exactly a function whose declared result is the domain.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
    function result does not prove declared type int[Digit]
    (facts: [-∞, +∞])
at the function (the column of its parameter list). The facts are the
range proven for the result when it is an integer. A `return` of another
type (`return "pos"` in an `-> int` function) is reported the same way,
at the function.

== WHAT TO DO
  * Guard the value: reject the out-of-range case first, with `fail` of a
    declared error or an early `return` of a value of the type, then
    produce the value (principle.guard-clauses).
  * Fix the branch or `return` that produces another type.
  * If the result really can be any int, declare `-> int`.

@example reject
type Digit = Int in 0..9

fn digit(n: int) -> Digit:
    n

digit(4)
@caption An unconstrained int does not prove Digit
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 3:9
@end

@example accept
type Digit = Int in 0..9

error NotADigit

fn digit(n: int) -> Digit errors NotADigit:
    if n < 0 or n > 9:
        fail NotADigit
    n

r = digit(4):
    on NotADigit:
        0
r
@caption A failing guard proves the result's range
@value 4
@end

@topic rule.type-field
title: A struct field or error payload field must be proven to have its declared type
summary: Constructing a struct, or failing with an error that carries a payload, requires each field value to be proven to have the field's declared type.
index: rules errors
alias: field cannot be proven to satisfy its declared type; payload field type
keywords: struct field, construction, payload, declared type, field type, fail
related: error.type rule.struct-construction concept.structs concept.error-payloads rule.type-argument
docs: STRUCTS.md ERROR-PAYLOADS.md
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | field "*" of struct * cannot be proven to satisfy its declared type *
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | payload field "*" of error * cannot be proven to satisfy its declared type *

== THE RULE
A struct declaration's field types (`value: Digit`) and an error
declaration's payload field types are held to the same proof as a typed
parameter: in `Cell {value: n}` or `fail Bad {code: n}`, each value must
be proven to have its field's declared type, by type or, for an integer
domain, by the proven range.

== WHY
A struct value carries no run-time type tags or checks: a reader of
`cell.value` relies on the declaration. So the one place the type is
established is the construction.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
    field "value" of struct Cell cannot be proven to satisfy its declared
    type int[Digit] (value type: int, facts: [-∞, +∞])
    payload field "code" of error Bad cannot be proven to satisfy its
    declared type ...
at the field's value.

== WHAT TO DO
As for an argument (rule.type-argument): declare the type where the
value comes from (`fn make(d: Digit) -> Cell`), guard an integer before
constructing, or convert the value.

@example reject
type Digit = Int in 0..9

struct Cell:
    value: Digit

fn make(n: int) -> Cell:
    Cell {value: n}

make(3)
@caption An unconstrained int for a Digit field
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 7:11
@end

@example reject
error Bad:
    code: int

fn check(s: str) -> int errors Bad:
    fail Bad {code: s}

0
@caption A payload field is checked like a struct field
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 5:15
@end

@example accept
type Digit = Int in 0..9

struct Cell:
    value: Digit

fn make(d: Digit) -> Cell:
    Cell {value: d}

make(3).value
@caption The parameter's declared type proves the field
@value 3
@end

@topic rule.type-annotation
title: A type annotation must name a type that exists here
summary: Type annotations name the built-in types (int, str, bool, unit, UnicodeChar, any, List[T], ...), declared types, structs and enums, or a qualified ns::T after an import.
index: rules errors
alias: unknown or invalid type; unknown type name
keywords: annotation, type name, int, str, bool, Int, String, List, unknown type, parent type, type constructor
related: error.type concept.types concept.imports concept.integer-domains concept.function-types rule.type-declaration
docs: MINIMAL-APPLIED-LIST-TYPES.md SOURCE-DEFINED-INTEGER-DOMAINS.md STRUCTURAL-FUNCTION-TYPES.md README.md
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | unknown or invalid *
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | unknown parent type *

== THE RULE
A parameter, result, field or context parameter type is one of:

    int  str  bool  unit  UnicodeChar  any       built-in value types
    List[T]  MutableVector[T]  MutableArray[T]  ImmutableSet[T]
    Fn{args: [...], return: T, errors: [...]}    function types
    Coroutine{...}
    NAME          a `type`, `refined type`, struct or enum declared in
                  this file, or imported with `import type ns::NAME`
    ns::NAME      a type, struct or enum of an imported namespace

The scalar types are spelled int, str, bool and unit: `Int`, `String`,
`Bool` and `string` are not types. `Int` appears only as the parent of
an integer domain (`type Digit = Int in 0..9`). A type constructor needs
its argument: `List` alone is invalid, `List[int]` is a type.

== WHY
A declared type is a proof obligation (error.type): it must denote
exactly one known set of values. An unknown name could only be guessed
at, so it is an error rather than `any`.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
    unknown or invalid type Int for parameter "x": core::type: unknown
    type "Int"
    unknown or invalid result type Foo: ...
    unknown or invalid type List for parameter "x": type constructor
    "List" requires 1 type argument(s) (e.g. List[...])
    unknown parent type "Foo"         (in `type T = Foo in ...`)

== WHAT TO DO
Spell the built-in type in lower case, give a constructor its element
type, declare the type before using it, or import its namespace and
spell it qualified. A struct of another module is spelled `ns::Name`
after `import ns` (`import type` does not import structs).

@example reject
fn half(x: Int) -> int:
    x

half(4)
@caption Int is not a type name: the integer type is int
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 1:8
@end

@example reject
type Digit = Integer in 0..9

0
@caption An unknown parent type
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 1:14
@end

@example accept
fn half(x: int) -> int:
    x

half(4)
@caption The built-in names are lower case
@value 4
@end

@topic rule.type-declaration
title: Type, struct and enum declarations: one name, distinct fields and cases
summary: Each type, struct, enum or layout name is declared once and is not a built-in type name; a struct's fields and an enum's cases are each declared once.
index: rules errors
alias: type is already declared; duplicate enum case; duplicate struct field declaration
keywords: declaration, already declared, duplicate case, duplicate field, built-in type, struct, enum, type, redeclare
related: error.type concept.structs concept.enums concept.integer-domains rule.type-annotation error.duplicate-field
docs: STRUCTS.md ENUMS.md SOURCE-DEFINED-INTEGER-DOMAINS.md
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | type "*" is already declared*
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | duplicate field "*" in struct "*"
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | duplicate case "*" in enum "*"*
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | "*" cannot be declared*
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | type declaration cycle: *

== THE RULE
Types, structs, enums, layouts and traits share one type namespace per
module: each name is declared once, and never as a built-in type name
(`int`, `List`, `any`, ...). Within one declaration, each struct field
and each enum case is declared once. An integer domain's chain of
parents must end in `Int`, without cycles.

== WHY
A type's identity is its declaration: two declarations of one name, or a
case declared twice, would make a name mean two things (Botlish has no
aliases or overloading of declarations).

== WHAT IS REPORTED
    type "P" is already declared
    duplicate field "x" in struct "P"
    duplicate case "Red" in enum "Color": every case of an enum is a
    distinct nominal case, declared once (there are no case aliases)
    "int" cannot be declared as a struct: the name is already a built-in
    or declared type
    type declaration cycle: "A" depends on its own declaration through
    its chain of parents
at the offending declaration. Two `error` declarations of one name are
reported as UNDECLARED-ERROR ("error "Boom" is already declared").

== WHAT TO DO
Rename one of the two declarations, or delete the copy.

@example reject
struct P:
    x: int

struct P:
    y: int

0
@caption Two structs named P
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 4:8
@end

@example reject
struct P:
    x: int
    x: int

0
@caption A struct field declared twice
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 3:5
@end

@example reject
enum Color:
    Red,
    Red,

0
@caption An enum case declared twice
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 3:5
@end

@example reject
struct int:
    x: int

0
@caption A built-in type name
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 1:8
@end

@example reject
type A = B in 0..3
type B = A in 0..3

0
@caption Each domain is the other's parent
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 1:6
@end

@topic error.duplicate-field
title: DUPLICATE-FIELD: one field given twice in a construction, payload or pattern
summary: A struct construction, an error payload declaration, a layout or a struct destructuring pattern names the same field twice; each field appears exactly once.
index: errors
alias: duplicate field; field given twice
keywords: duplicate field, construction, destructuring, payload, layout, field twice
related: rule.struct-construction concept.structs concept.destructuring concept.error-payloads concept.binary-layouts rule.type-declaration error.missing-field
docs: STRUCTS.md STRUCT-DESTRUCTURING.md ERROR-PAYLOADS.md BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE-FIELD
code: DUPLICATE-FIELD
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | DUPLICATE-FIELD: *

== MEANING
Fields are named, and each is given exactly once: a construction gives
every declared field one value, a destructuring pattern selects each
field once, a payload or layout declares each field once. A second
occurrence could only override or contradict the first, and Botlish has
neither defaults nor overriding.

    duplicate field "x" in the P construction: each field may be given
    only once
    duplicate field "code" in the payload of error "Bad": each payload
    field is declared once
    duplicate field "tag" in layout "Header" (first declared at ...)
    DUPLICATE-FIELD: duplicate field "x" in this struct destructuring:
    each field may be selected only once        (SURFACE SYNTAX)

The last is found by the parser, so it is printed as SURFACE SYNTAX with
the parser code DUPLICATE-FIELD at the start of its message. A field
declared twice in a `struct` declaration is reported as TYPE
(rule.type-declaration).

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A copied `name: value` line in a construction.
  * A destructuring that selects one field under two local names:
    `{x, x: other}` -- write `{x}` and bind `other = x` afterwards.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The location is the second occurrence; the first is earlier between the
same braces (for a layout, the message gives its location).

== WHAT TO DO
Delete the repeated field. To use one field value under two names,
select it once and bind the second name to the first.

@example reject
struct P:
    x: int
    y: int

P {x: 1, x: 2, y: 3}
@caption x is given twice
@error CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE-FIELD
@at 5:10
@end

@example reject
struct P:
    x: int
    y: int

fn f(p: P) -> int:
    {x, x} = p
    x

f(P {x: 1, y: 2})
@caption A pattern selects x twice (parser)
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 6:9
@end

@topic error.missing-field
title: MISSING-FIELD: a construction or payload that leaves out a declared field
summary: A struct construction, or an error payload in fail, omits a field the declaration has; every declared field must be given exactly once, and there are no defaults.
index: errors
alias: missing field; field not given; default field value
keywords: missing field, construction, payload, fail, defaults, required field
related: rule.struct-construction concept.structs concept.error-payloads error.unknown-field error.duplicate-field
docs: STRUCTS.md ERROR-PAYLOADS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-FIELD

== MEANING
A named struct value is built with every declared field:
`P {x: 1, y: 2}` for `struct P` with fields x and y. There are no
default values, no optional fields and no partial constructions, so a
construction that leaves a field out has no value for it. The same holds
for the payload of `fail E {...}`: it gives every field `error E:`
declares.

    the P construction is missing field "y" (every declared field must
    be given exactly once; there are no defaults)
    the payload of error Bad is missing field "text" (...)

The raw Linux syscall transports take a struct of registers; leaving out
one they require is reported the same way.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A field added to the struct after the constructions were written.
  * A struct used like a record with optional fields.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Each missing field is a diagnostic of its own, naming the construction's
type and the field; compare the construction with the `struct`
declaration.

== WHAT TO DO
Give the field a value. When many constructions share values, write one
function that builds the struct (a constructor function with the common
values written once) and call it.

@example reject
struct P:
    x: int
    y: int

P {x: 1}
@caption y is not given
@error CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-FIELD
@at 5:1
@end

@example accept
struct P:
    x: int
    y: int

fn on_x_axis(x: int) -> P:
    P {x: x, y: 0}

on_x_axis(4).x
@caption A function supplies the common value
@value 4
@end

@topic error.unknown-field
title: UNKNOWN-FIELD: a field the struct or payload does not declare
summary: A construction gives, or a projection or pattern reads, a field the struct type (or the error payload) does not have.
index: errors
alias: no such field; struct has no field
keywords: unknown field, field access, projection, construction, payload, typo, method syntax
related: concept.structs rule.struct-construction concept.method-sugar concept.error-payloads error.missing-field error.not-a-struct error.missing-import
docs: STRUCTS.md METHOD-SUGAR.md ERROR-PAYLOADS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-FIELD

== MEANING
Fields are resolved statically, by the struct type the compiler proves
for the value; there is no field lookup at run time. A field name the
struct's declaration does not have is rejected wherever it is written:

    struct P has no field "z" (declared fields: x)
        a construction `P {x: 1, z: 2}`;
    struct type P has no field "z" (known fields: x)
        a projection `p.z` or a destructuring `{z} = p` (in a handler
        pattern `on Bad {nope}:` the type is the payload's anonymous
        struct type, `struct{code: int}`);
    the payload of error Bad has no field "extra" (declared fields: code)
        a payload in `fail Bad {...}`.

A method-style call `p.size()` is a field call when no function `size`
is visible, so a missing function can surface here; the message then
adds: "as a method-style call, no function named "size" is visible here
either ...". When such a function exists in a namespace the file does
not import, the diagnostic is MISSING-IMPORT instead.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A typo in the field name, or a field renamed in the declaration.
  * A method call whose function is not defined, or is defined after the
    call (functions are visible only from their definition on).
  * The wrong struct: two structs with similar fields.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Compare the name with the field list in the message. When the code is
a method call (`p.size()`), the question is instead which function
`size` was meant, whether it is defined above this line, and whether its
namespace is imported.

== WHAT TO DO
Use a declared field (the message lists them), add the field to the
declaration (and to every construction), or define the function the
method call meant before the call.

@example reject
struct P:
    x: int

p = P {x: 1}
p.z
@caption P has no field z
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-FIELD
@at 5:3
@end

@topic error.unknown-struct
title: UNKNOWN-STRUCT: a construction of a name that is not a struct type
summary: A construction NAME {...} names no struct declaration visible here, or names a type that is not a struct (an integer domain, an applied type, a layout).
index: errors
alias: unknown struct type; not a struct type
keywords: construction, struct, unknown struct, braces, layout, LayoutStruct, domain
related: concept.structs rule.struct-construction concept.imports concept.binary-layouts error.not-a-struct
docs: STRUCTS.md BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-STRUCT

== MEANING
`NAME {field: value, ...}` constructs a value of the struct NAME, which
must be declared (`struct NAME:`) in this file, or be `ns::NAME` of an
imported module. Only structs are constructed from fields:

    unknown struct type "Q": no "struct Q" declaration is visible
    "Small" is not a struct type              (an integer domain)
    List[int] is not a struct type: of the applied types, only
    LayoutStruct[L] is constructed from field values
    "Header" is a layout, not a struct: its structural value is
    constructed as LayoutStruct[Header] {...}

An anonymous struct value needs no declaration: `{x: 1, y: 2}`.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A typo, or a struct of another module written without its qualifier
    (`Point {...}` for `geo::Point {...}`).
  * Not the order: unlike value bindings, struct declarations are
    visible in the whole file, so a construction above the declaration
    is fine.
  * A value of an integer domain written like a construction:
    `Small {value: 3}` -- a domain value is just the integer, produced by
    a function whose declared result is the domain.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Search the file for `struct NAME`. For a struct of a module, check the
qualifier names that module and that the file imports it.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the struct, qualify it (`ns::Name {...}` after `import ns`), or
use an anonymous struct `{...}` if no named type is needed.

@example reject
Q {x: 1}
@caption No struct Q is declared
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-STRUCT
@at 1:1
@end

@topic error.not-a-struct
title: NOT-A-STRUCT: a field read from a value that is not a struct
summary: A field projection or destructuring reads a field of a value whose type is not a struct (an int, a str, a List ...), or braces follow an enum case.
index: errors
alias: only a struct has fields; cannot project field
keywords: projection, field, dot, struct, method syntax, destructuring, enum case braces
related: concept.structs concept.method-sugar concept.enums error.unknown-field error.missing-import rule.method-requires-import
docs: STRUCTS.md METHOD-SUGAR.md DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md ENUMS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC NOT-A-STRUCT
code: SURFACE MODULE NOT-A-STRUCT

== MEANING
`value.name` reads field `name` of a struct value. For a value whose
type is known not to be a struct, there is no field to read:

    cannot project field "size" from a value of type int: only a struct
    has fields

The same holds for a destructuring `{a} = n` of a non-struct. The loader
reports one more case (SURFACE MODULE NOT-A-STRUCT): braces after an
enum case, `Color::Red {}` -- a case is a value, written without braces,
and cases carry no payload.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Method syntax with no function of that name visible: `n.size()` is a
    call of the field `size` when no function `size` is in scope. The
    message then adds "as a method-style call, no function named "size"
    is visible here either". Method syntax applies only functions
    visible by name (concept.method-sugar). If such a function exists in
    a namespace this file does not import, the diagnostic is
    MISSING-IMPORT, naming the import to add.
  * A wrong assumption about a value's type: a function returning a str
    where a struct was expected.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message gives the value's type. For a method call, find the
function that was meant: is it defined in this file, above the call? Is
its namespace imported? If the type itself is unexpected, trace where
the value comes from.

== WHAT TO DO
Define or import the function the method call meant, call a function
explicitly (`size(n)`), or fix the value's type.

@example reject
n = 5
n.size()
@caption A field call on an int: no function size
@error CORE SEMANTIC NOT-A-STRUCT
@at 2:3
@end

@example reject
enum Color:
    Red,
    Green,

Color::Red {}
@caption An enum case is a value: no braces
@error SURFACE MODULE NOT-A-STRUCT
@at 5:1
@end

@topic error.unproven-field
title: UNPROVEN-FIELD: a field read whose struct type is not known statically
summary: A field is read from a value of unknown type (an untyped parameter) in code that can run with any argument, so no struct and no field can be chosen at compile time.
index: errors
alias: receiver's struct type is not known; field of untyped parameter
keywords: generic, untyped parameter, field projection, any, function value, higher-order, instance
related: concept.specialization concept.structs rule.type-generic-call concept.function-types error.not-a-struct
docs: STRUCTS.md OPPORTUNISTIC-SEMANTIC-INSTANCES.md DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC UNPROVEN-FIELD

== MEANING
A field projection is compiled to a fixed slot of a known struct type;
there is no field lookup by name at run time. In an untyped (generic)
function, `fn first(x): x.value`, the type of x is unknown in the body
itself. That is fine while every call of the function is a direct call
the compiler analyzes with the argument's actual type (a semantic
instance: `first(Box {value: 3})` proves x is a Box). It is an error
when the generic body itself can run with an argument of any type:

  * the function is used as a value -- passed to another function,
    stored in a list, bound to another name and called through it;
  * or it is called with an argument whose type is itself unknown.

    cannot project field "value": the receiver's struct type is not
    known here (its type is any), and a field projection is resolved
    statically, never looked up at run time

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A generic accessor passed to a higher-order function:
    `apply(first, box)`.
  * A field read on an untyped parameter of a function that is called
    with values of unknown type.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The location is the projection. Find where the function escapes as a
value (an argument, a list element, a binding `f = first`).

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the parameter's struct type (`fn first(x: Box) -> int`): the
projection is then resolved in every context, including through
function values. Alternatively call the function directly, where the
argument's type is known.

@example reject
fn first(x):
    x.value

fn apply(f, v):
    f(v)

struct Box:
    value: int

apply(first, Box {value: 3})
@caption first escapes as a value
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNPROVEN-FIELD
@at 2:7
@end

@example accept
struct Box:
    value: int

fn first(x: Box) -> int:
    x.value

fn apply(v, f):
    f(v)

Box {value: 3}.apply(first)
@caption A declared type resolves the field
@value 3
@end

@topic error.opaque-construction
title: OPAQUE-CONSTRUCTION: an opaque struct built outside its module
summary: A struct declared opaque struct can be constructed from its fields only by the module that declares it; other code must call that module's functions.
index: errors
alias: opaque struct construction; opaque representation owned by
keywords: opaque, construction, representation, module, owner, abstraction
related: concept.opaque-structs rule.opaque-authority error.opaque-representation concept.modules concept.structs
docs: OPAQUE-STRUCTS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC OPAQUE-CONSTRUCTION

== MEANING
`opaque struct Name:` in module m makes m the only code with authority
over the struct's representation: only m can build a value from fields
or read its fields. Everyone else may name the type, pass, store and
compare its values, and call m's functions.

    std::abi::bytes::Bytes has an opaque representation owned by module
    "std::abi::bytes"; construct it through the public operations provided by
    that module

The diagnostic deliberately says nothing about the fields (which exist,
which are missing): they are the owner's business.

== WHY
The owner can keep an invariant (a validated value, a private encoding)
that a construction from arbitrary field values would break, and can
change the representation without breaking other modules. Imports grant
no authority: no parent, child or importing namespace may construct.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Building a library type from its fields, as for a plain struct.
  * Code moved out of the module that owns the type.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names the owning module. Its functions are the interface:
`-help stdlib.module.NS` lists a standard module's members.

== WHAT TO DO
Use the module's constructor functions (`std::abi::bytes::from_list(...)`);
`-help stdlib.module.NS` lists a module's functions. If you own the
module and need another construction, add a function there.

@example reject
import std::abi::bytes

std::abi::bytes::Bytes {storage: 0}
@caption Only std::abi::bytes may build a Bytes
@error CORE SEMANTIC OPAQUE-CONSTRUCTION
@at 3:1
@end

@topic error.opaque-representation
title: OPAQUE-REPRESENTATION: a field of an opaque struct read outside its module
summary: A field projection or destructuring of an opaque struct value outside the module that declares it; only the owning module may inspect the representation.
index: errors
alias: field access on opaque struct; opaque fields
keywords: opaque, field access, projection, destructuring, representation, owner
related: concept.opaque-structs rule.opaque-authority error.opaque-construction concept.destructuring
docs: OPAQUE-STRUCTS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC OPAQUE-REPRESENTATION

== MEANING
Outside its owning module an opaque struct's fields can be neither
projected (`data.storage`) nor destructured (`{storage} = data`), and
they are not method-call candidates. The message is the same whether the
field exists or not, so the representation cannot be probed:

    field access on std::abi::bytes::Bytes is not available outside module
    "std::abi::bytes"; std::abi::bytes::Bytes has an opaque representation, so its
    fields can be neither projected nor destructured here (use the public
    operations provided by that module)

== WHY
See error.opaque-construction: the owner alone controls the
representation, so it can keep invariants and change it freely.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Reading a field instead of calling an accessor function.
  * Destructuring an opaque value: `{storage} = data`.
  * A method call `data.f()` whose function f is not visible here:
    without a function, method syntax reads the field f.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names the owning module; look for an accessor among its
functions (`-help stdlib.module.NS` for a standard module).

== WHAT TO DO
Call the owning module's accessor functions
(`std::abi::bytes::length(data)`, or `data.length()` with method syntax
after the import).

@example reject
import std::abi::bytes

fn storage_of(data: std::abi::bytes::Bytes) -> any:
    data.storage

0
@caption A field of an opaque struct
@error CORE SEMANTIC OPAQUE-REPRESENTATION
@at 4:10
@end

@topic error.private-access
title: PRIVATE-ACCESS: a private declaration used outside its module
summary: Code of one module names, calls or method-calls a declaration another module declared `private`; only the declaring module may use it.
index: errors
alias: is private to module; private function; cannot be accessed from
keywords: private, access, module, visibility, method syntax, helper
related: concept.private concept.modules concept.opaque-structs error.private-type-escape error.missing-import
docs: MODULE-PRIVACY-AND-CONTEXT-ESCAPE-MILESTONE-1.md
code: SURFACE MODULE PRIVATE-ACCESS
code: CORE SEMANTIC PRIVATE-ACCESS

== MEANING
The declaration exists, and the file imports its namespace, but it is
written `private` in its own module: no other module may name it, call it,
take it as a value, use it as a type, construct it, read its fields, fail
with it or handle it. The message names the declaring module:

    gfx::scale is private to module "gfx" and cannot be accessed from the
    entry program: a private function is accessible only to code of the
    module that declares it

A method-style call `x.scale()` whose only function of that name is a
private one of an imported module is the same access.

== WHY
`private` is the module author's statement that the declaration is an
implementation detail, so it can change or disappear without breaking
any other module (concept.private).

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Calling a helper of a module instead of its public operation.
  * Copying code out of a module into its importer.
  * A method call `x.f()` that used to find a public `f`.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The related location is the private declaration. Look for the public
operation the module offers for the same purpose.

== WHAT TO DO
Use the module's public interface. If the declaration is genuinely part of
the interface, the module's author removes `private`.

@example reject
import gfx

gfx::scale(2)
@module gfx
private fn scale(x: int) -> int:
    x * 10

fn draw(x: int) -> int:
    scale(x) + 1
@caption The helper is the module's own
@error SURFACE MODULE PRIVATE-ACCESS
@at 3:1
@end

@topic error.private-type-escape
title: PRIVATE-TYPE-ESCAPE: a public declaration exposes a private type
summary: A public function, struct, error, trait, layout, type or binding names a private type or error of its module in its interface (or infers one), which no other module could name or use.
index: errors
alias: exposes private type; private type in public signature
keywords: private, public, signature, interface, opaque, inferred result, context requirement
related: concept.private error.private-access concept.opaque-structs
docs: MODULE-PRIVACY-AND-CONTEXT-ESCAPE-MILESTONE-1.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC PRIVATE-TYPE-ESCAPE

== MEANING
A public declaration's interface is what other modules use: a function's
parameter types, its declared or inferred result, its context requirements
(a caller must install them), its errors; a non-opaque struct's fields; an
error's payload; a trait's requirements; a layout's fields. One of them
holds a type or error the module declared `private`:

    public function gfx::handles exposes private type gfx::Handles in its
    result: every module may use gfx::handles, but only module "gfx" may
    use gfx::Handles

== WHY
Another module could reach the function but never name, build or handle
what it takes or returns: an interface no caller can use, or a private
type smuggled out of its module.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A helper that returns an internal record was left public.
  * A public struct holds an internal record in an open field.
  * A public function requires a private context.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names the declaration, the private type and where in the
interface it appears.

== WHAT TO DO
Make the declaration private too, or keep the private type out of its
interface: return values instead, or hold it in an opaque struct, whose
representation is not interface.

@example reject
import gfx

0
@module gfx
private struct Handles:
    renderer: int

fn handles() -> Handles:
    Handles {renderer: 1}
@caption A public function returning a private struct
@error CORE SEMANTIC PRIVATE-TYPE-ESCAPE
@at gfx.bot:4:1
@end

@example accept
import gfx

gfx::renderer(gfx::open())
@module gfx
private struct Handles:
    renderer: int

opaque struct Session:
    handles: Handles

fn open() -> Session:
    Session {handles: Handles {renderer: 4}}

fn renderer(s: Session) -> int:
    s.handles.renderer
@caption Hidden in an opaque struct, the private record is no interface
@value 4
@end

@topic error.unknown-enum-case
title: UNKNOWN-ENUM-CASE: Enum::Case naming a case the enum does not declare
summary: A qualified case Enum::Case names a case the enum's declaration does not have; the message lists the declared cases and suggests near spellings.
index: errors
alias: no such enum case; enum has no case
keywords: enum, case, typo, did you mean, closed sum
related: concept.enums error.not-an-enum error.ambiguous-qualifier
docs: ENUMS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-ENUM-CASE

== MEANING
An enum is a closed set of named cases, all listed in its declaration.
`Color::Blue` is valid only if `enum Color:` declares `Blue`; cases
cannot be added elsewhere and have no numeric or string conversion.

    enum Color has no case "Gren" (its cases: Red, Green); did you mean
    Color::Green or Color::Red?

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A typo or a renamed case.
  * The wrong enum: two enums with similar cases.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message lists the enum's cases and the nearest spellings; check the
enum's declaration if a case was renamed.

== WHAT TO DO
Use a declared case (the message lists them), or add the case to the
declaration.

@example reject
enum Color:
    Red,
    Green,

Color::Gren
@caption A misspelled case
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-ENUM-CASE
@at 5:1
@end

@topic error.not-an-enum
title: NOT-AN-ENUM: Type::Case qualified by a type that is not an enum
summary: A case-like reference Name::Case is qualified by a struct or built-in type; only an enum type qualifies a case, and a type is never a namespace.
index: errors
alias: not an enum; only an enum type qualifies a case
keywords: enum, case, qualifier, struct, built-in type, namespace
related: concept.enums error.unknown-enum-case error.unknown-namespace error.ambiguous-qualifier
docs: ENUMS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE NOT-AN-ENUM

== MEANING
`X::Y` where X is not a namespace is read as an enum case: X must be an
enum type visible in this file. When X is another kind of type, the
reference names nothing:

    "bool" is a built-in type, not an enum: only an enum type qualifies a
    case ("Enum::Case"), and a type is not a namespace, so "bool::Yes"
    names nothing
    "P" is a struct, not an enum: ...

Types are not namespaces in Botlish: a struct has no static members, and
`P::make` is never a function of P.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Expecting static members or associated constants on a struct.
  * A boolean written as an enum (`bool::True` for `true`).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Check what X is: a struct, a built-in type or a `type` declaration. If
a namespace's member was meant, the namespace has another name (and
needs an import).

== WHAT TO DO
Declare an enum for a closed set of cases, write a plain function
instead of `P::make` (`make_p(...)`), or use `true`/`false`.

@example reject
bool::Yes
@caption bool is a type, not an enum
@error SURFACE MODULE NOT-AN-ENUM
@at 1:1
@end

@topic error.enum-empty
title: ENUM-EMPTY: an enum declaration without cases
summary: An enum declares no case; an enum needs at least one case on the indented lines after enum Name:.
index: errors
alias: enum without cases; empty enum
keywords: enum, case, declaration, empty
related: concept.enums error.anonymous-enum
docs: ENUMS.md
code: ENUM-EMPTY
code: CORE SEMANTIC ENUM-EMPTY
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | ENUM-EMPTY: *

== MEANING
An enum's values are its cases, so an enum with no case would be a type
with no values. The parser rejects it:

    ENUM-EMPTY: enum "Color" declares no case: an enum needs at least one
    case, written on the indented lines after "enum Color:" ("Case,")

The cases are one comma-separated list on indented lines:

    enum Color:
        Red,
        Green,

HIR text (a `.hirx` file) that declares an empty enum is rejected as
CORE SEMANTIC ENUM-EMPTY when it is read; a source program never reaches
that check.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A declaration written before its cases.
  * Cases that are not indented under `enum Name:` (they are then not
    part of the declaration).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The location is the enum's name; look at the lines below it.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the cases, indented under the declaration, separated by commas.

@example reject
enum Color:

Color::Red
@caption No indented case list
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:6
@end

@topic error.anonymous-enum
title: ANONYMOUS-ENUM: enum {...} used as an expression or type
summary: An anonymous enum type such as enum {A, B} does not exist; an enum is always a named top-level declaration.
index: errors
alias: anonymous enum; inline enum
keywords: enum, anonymous, braces, declaration
related: concept.enums error.enum-empty
docs: ENUMS.md
code: ANONYMOUS-ENUM
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | ANONYMOUS-ENUM: *

== MEANING
An enum's identity is its declaration: two enums with the same cases are
different types, and `Color::Red` is a case of exactly one of them. A
type written in place, `enum {Red, Green}`, would have no identity, so
the parser rejects it:

    ANONYMOUS-ENUM: anonymous enum types do not exist: an enum's identity
    is its declaration, so an enum is always a named top-level
    declaration ("enum Name:" with its cases on indented lines), and a
    value of it is written Name::Case

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * An enum written inline, as some languages allow for a field's type
    or a value: `kind: enum {A, B}`, `x = enum {A, B}`.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The location is the word `enum` before the brace.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the enum at the top level of the file and use its cases:

    enum Color:
        Red,
        Green,

    c = Color::Red

@example reject
c = enum {Red, Green}
c
@caption An enum written in place
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:5
@end

@topic error.ambiguous-qualifier
title: AMBIGUOUS-QUALIFIER: a qualifier that is both a namespace and an enum
summary: X::Y is ambiguous because X names both a namespace and an enum type visible in this file; qualifiers are never resolved by precedence.
index: errors
alias: namespace and enum with the same name
keywords: enum, namespace, qualifier, ambiguous, rename
related: concept.enums concept.imports error.not-an-enum error.unknown-namespace
docs: ENUMS.md IMPORTS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE AMBIGUOUS-QUALIFIER

== MEANING
`X::Y` is a namespace member when X is a namespace and an enum case when
X is an enum type. When an enum is named like a namespace that exists --
one of the program's own modules, a file NAME.bot beside its entry
program -- the reference has two meanings, and Botlish never picks one by
a precedence rule:

    "game::Red" is ambiguous: "game" names both a namespace and the enum
    type game visible in this file (a qualifier is never resolved by
    precedence; rename the enum)

This is reported whether or not the file imports the namespace, so a
qualified name never means a module's member in one file and a case in
another. A standard namespace never meets an enum this way: its path
starts at the root std (std::list), which no enum's name can, and an
enum named like a built-in type (`list`) is rejected as a declaration
first. The diagnostic needs a module file beside the program, so there
is no single-file example here; the message above comes from two files
in one directory:

    main.bot              game.bot
      enum game:            fn f() -> int:
          Red,                  1

      game::Red

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * An enum named in lower case like one of the program's own modules
    (`game` beside game.bot).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names the namespace and the enum: the namespace is the
application module NAME.bot beside the entry program.

== WHAT TO DO
Rename the enum (enum types are conventionally capitalized: `Game`,
`Status`), so no module shares its name.

@topic error.ambiguous-method-call
title: AMBIGUOUS-METHOD-CALL: method syntax with more than one possible callee
summary: receiver.name(...) could call more than one visible function named name (or a function and a callable field), and Botlish never chooses by precedence; call the one you mean explicitly.
index: errors
alias: ambiguous method call; more than one visible function accepts the receiver
keywords: method syntax, method sugar, candidates, ambiguous, precedence, field, local name, parameter name, shadowing
related: concept.method-sugar rule.method-resolution error.no-applicable-method error.nomethod-call concept.imports
docs: METHOD-SUGAR.md IMPORTS.md 2048-ENGINE-WORKLOAD.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AMBIGUOUS-METHOD-CALL

== MEANING
`x.f(a)` means `f(x, a)` for a function `f` visible by that name. The
candidates are every `f` lexical resolution finds (functions, local
bindings and parameters of this file) plus every member `f` of a
namespace the file imports directly. Each candidate is tried as
`f(x, a)`; the call is accepted when exactly one is valid. When several
are, the call is rejected -- there is no precedence: not import order,
not "local wins", not "standard library wins":

    method-style call "length" is ambiguous: more than one visible
    function accepts the receiver (length, std::list::length); there is no
    precedence between them: call the one you mean explicitly, e.g.
    length(receiver, ...)

A second form: the receiver is a struct with a field `f` that may hold a
callable, and a function `f` is also visible:

    method-style call "greet" is ambiguous: the receiver (type Greeter)
    has a field "greet" whose value may be callable, and a function
    "greet" is also visible here; write (receiver.greet)(...) to call the
    field, or greet(receiver, ...) to call the function

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A local binding or parameter with the method's name: with
    `import std::list`, a parameter `length: int` makes `xs.length()`
    ambiguous. Today a local of any type counts as a candidate, even an
    Int that could never be called.
  * A helper function of your own named like an imported function
    (`fn length(...)` beside `import std::list`).
  * Two imported namespaces with a function of that name that both
    accept the receiver (e.g. an untyped receiver).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message lists the candidates (`length, std::list::length`). For each,
find where it comes from: a parameter or local binding, a function of
this file, or a namespace this file imports.

== WHAT TO DO
  * Rename the local or parameter (`count`, `size_limit`), or your own
    function.
  * Or call explicitly: `std::list::length(xs)` or `length(xs)`, and for a
    field `(x.f)(a)`.
  * Give an untyped receiver a declared type, so only one candidate
    accepts it.

@example reject
import std::list

fn count(xs: List[int], length: int) -> int:
    xs.length() + length

count([1, 2], 3)
@caption A parameter named like std::list::length
@error CORE SEMANTIC AMBIGUOUS-METHOD-CALL
@at 4:8
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn count(xs: List[int], limit: int) -> int:
    xs.length() + limit

[1, 2].count(3)
@caption Renamed: std::list::length is the only candidate
@value 5
@end

@topic error.no-applicable-method
title: NO-APPLICABLE-METHOD: method syntax where no candidate accepts the receiver
summary: receiver.name(...) has several visible functions named name, and none of them accepts this receiver and these arguments; the message says why each one fails.
index: errors
alias: no visible function accepts the receiver
keywords: method syntax, candidates, receiver type, overload, no applicable
related: concept.method-sugar rule.method-resolution error.ambiguous-method-call error.type
docs: METHOD-SUGAR.md IMPORTS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC NO-APPLICABLE-METHOD

== MEANING
When method syntax has several candidate functions (see
error.ambiguous-method-call), each is tried with the receiver as first
argument. If none of the calls would be valid, the call is rejected, and
the message gives each candidate's reason:

    method-style call "length": none of the visible functions of that
    name accepts the receiver (length: argument for parameter "s" cannot
    be proven to satisfy str (argument type: int); std::list::length: the
    receiver is a int, but argument 1 of native std::list::length requires
    list)

Botlish has no overloading: candidates are not ranked or converted to,
they are only tried. With a single candidate there is nothing to choose:
the call is that function's call, checked like any other (a typed
parameter rejects a wrong receiver as TYPE at compile time; an intrinsic
checks its argument's kind when it runs).

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A receiver of the wrong type: an int where a list was meant.
  * Your own function and an imported one sharing a name, neither for
    this receiver.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message gives each candidate's reason, separated by ";". The
receiver's type is in the reasons ("the receiver is a int").

== WHAT TO DO
Read each candidate's reason; fix the receiver's type or call the
intended function explicitly.

@example reject
import std::list

fn length(s: str) -> int:
    0

5.length()
@caption Neither length nor std::list::length accepts an int
@error CORE SEMANTIC NO-APPLICABLE-METHOD
@at 6:3
@end

@topic error.nomethod-call
title: NOMETHOD-CALL: method syntax on a function declared nomethod
summary: The only visible functions of that name are declared nomethod, so they cannot be called with method syntax; call them in function form.
index: errors
alias: declared nomethod; cannot be called with method syntax
keywords: nomethod, method syntax, receiver, bit_and, function form
related: concept.nomethod concept.method-sugar warning.method-eligible error.ambiguous-method-call
docs: WARNINGS-METHOD-ELIGIBLE.md METHOD-SUGAR.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC NOMETHOD-CALL

== MEANING
`nomethod fn f(...)` is the author's declaration that f has no receiver:
its first argument is not "the thing it operates on" (`bit_and`'s two
operands are symmetric; `std::mutable_array::create`'s first argument is a
length). Method syntax skips such a function as a candidate; when every
visible function of the name is nomethod, the call is rejected:

    `bit_and` is declared nomethod and cannot be called with method
    syntax; call it as bit_and(receiver, ...)

It is not a warning and has no suppression: nomethod changes which call
forms are legal. The standard library's nomethod functions include bit_and,
bit_or, bit_xor, std::mutable_array::copy, std::mutable_array::create and
std::binary::parse.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A symmetric operation or a constructor-like function written in
    receiver form: `a.bit_and(b)`, `3.create(0)` for
    std::mutable_array::create.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
For a standard function, `-help NAME` says whether it is nomethod; for
your own, look for `nomethod fn` at its declaration.

== WHAT TO DO
Write the function form: `bit_and(12, 10)`. If you own the function and
receiver syntax reads well for it, remove `nomethod`.

@example reject
12.bit_and(10)
@caption bit_and is declared nomethod
@error CORE SEMANTIC NOMETHOD-CALL
@at 1:4
@end

@example accept
bit_and(12, 10)
@caption The function form
@value 8
@end

@topic error.unknown-flag
title: UNKNOWN-FLAG: a flag the called function does not declare
summary: A call supplies :name, but the called function's flag section does not declare that flag (or the function declares no flags).
index: errors
alias: does not accept flag; unknown flag
keywords: flag, flags, :name, option, flag section
related: concept.flags rule.flags error.duplicate-flag error.flag-callee-unknown principle.options-are-flags
docs: FLAGS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-FLAG

== MEANING
A flag parameter is an option declared in the function's flag section
(`fn emit(x, flags :quiet):`) and set by naming it at the call
(`emit(x, :quiet)`). A call may supply only flags its callee declares:

    function emit does not accept flag :loud (accepted flags: :quiet)
    function plain does not accept flag :loud (it declares no flags)

The flag interface is the declaration's, checked statically at every
call; a flag is never a value or a string looked up at run time.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A typo in the flag name, or a flag of another function.
  * An option the function takes as an ordinary Bool parameter, not as
    a flag.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message lists the accepted flags; read the function's flag section
(`flags :a, :b` after its ordinary parameters).

== WHAT TO DO
Use a declared flag (the message lists them) or add it to the function's
flag section.

@example reject
fn emit(x, flags :quiet):
    x

emit(1, :loud)
@caption emit declares :quiet only
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-FLAG
@at 4:9
@end

@topic error.duplicate-flag
title: DUPLICATE-FLAG: one flag supplied twice in a call
summary: A call names the same flag twice; a flag is either present or absent, so each is written at most once.
index: errors
alias: flag supplied more than once
keywords: flag, flags, duplicate, call
related: concept.flags rule.flags error.unknown-flag error.duplicate-flag-declaration
docs: FLAGS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE-FLAG

== MEANING
A flag is a Bool that is true exactly when the call names it. Naming it
twice means nothing more, so it is rejected as a likely mistake:

    flag :quiet was supplied more than once

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Copy and paste, or two flags meant to be different.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The location is the second spelling of the flag.

== WHAT TO DO
Write each flag once.

@example reject
fn emit(x, flags :quiet):
    x

emit(1, :quiet, :quiet)
@caption :quiet is written twice
@error CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE-FLAG
@at 4:17
@end

@topic error.duplicate-flag-declaration
title: DUPLICATE-FLAG-DECLARATION: one flag declared twice in a flag section
summary: A function's flag section declares the same flag name twice.
index: errors
alias: flag declared more than once
keywords: flag, flags, declaration, flag section, duplicate
related: concept.flags rule.flags error.flag-binding-collision error.duplicate
docs: FLAGS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE-FLAG-DECLARATION

== MEANING
Each flag of a function binds a local Bool of its name (`quiet` for
`:quiet`), so a flag section declares each name once:

    flag :quiet is declared more than once in the same flag section

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Copy and paste in the flag section.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The location is the second declaration of the flag.

== WHAT TO DO
Delete the repeated declaration, or rename it if two options were meant.

@example reject
fn emit(x, flags :quiet, :quiet):
    x

emit(1)
@caption :quiet is declared twice
@error CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE-FLAG-DECLARATION
@at 1:26
@end

@topic error.flag-binding-collision
title: FLAG-BINDING-COLLISION: a flag named like an ordinary parameter
summary: A flag binds a local with its name, and an ordinary parameter of the same function already has that name.
index: errors
alias: flag named like a parameter
keywords: flag, flags, parameter, local name, collision
related: concept.flags rule.flags error.duplicate-flag-declaration error.duplicate
docs: FLAGS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC FLAG-BINDING-COLLISION

== MEANING
In the body, flag `:quiet` is the Bool local `quiet`. Ordinary
parameters and flags share the function's one local namespace, so they
cannot share a name:

    flag :quiet binds the local name "quiet", which is already a
    parameter of this function: ordinary parameters and flags share one
    local namespace

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A Bool option first written as an ordinary parameter and then added
    as a flag too.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The location is the flag; the parameter of the same name is earlier in
the parameter list.

== WHAT TO DO
Rename the parameter or the flag.

@example reject
fn emit(quiet, flags :quiet):
    quiet

emit(1)
@caption The parameter and the flag are both quiet
@error CORE SEMANTIC FLAG-BINDING-COLLISION
@at 1:22
@end

@topic error.flag-callee-unknown
title: FLAG-CALLEE-UNKNOWN: a flag supplied to a callee that is not a known function
summary: A flag is written in a call whose callee is not statically a declared function (a parameter, a field, a computed value), so its flag interface is unknown.
index: errors
alias: cannot supply flag to a function value
keywords: flag, flags, function value, parameter, higher-order, callee
related: concept.flags rule.flags error.flag-function-value concept.function-types
docs: FLAGS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC FLAG-CALLEE-UNKNOWN

== MEANING
Flags are resolved against the called declaration at compile time. A
callee that is a parameter or another computed value has no declaration
the compiler can see, so it cannot accept flags:

    cannot supply flag :quiet: the callee is not a function whose
    declaration is statically known (flags can only be supplied to a
    call of a declared function, or of a name bound to one)

Function types (`Fn{...}`) have no flag interface.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A flag passed through a callback parameter, a struct field holding a
    function, or a function chosen at run time.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Look at what the callee expression is: a declared function, a name
bound to one, or a value of unknown identity.

== WHAT TO DO
Call the declared function directly (or through a name bound to it,
`say = emit`), or pass the option as an ordinary Bool argument where a
function value is needed.

@example reject
fn apply(f, x):
    f(x, :quiet)

fn id(x):
    x

apply(id, 1)
@caption Flags on a call through a parameter
@error CORE SEMANTIC FLAG-CALLEE-UNKNOWN
@at 2:10
@end

@topic error.flag-function-value
title: FLAG-FUNCTION-VALUE: a function with flags used as a value
summary: A function that declares flags can only be called or bound to another name; passing, returning or storing it would lose its flag interface.
index: errors
alias: function with flags as a value
keywords: flag, flags, function value, higher-order, alias, argument
related: concept.flags rule.flags error.flag-callee-unknown concept.function-types
docs: FLAGS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC FLAG-FUNCTION-VALUE

== MEANING
A function's flags belong to its declaration; no function type carries a
flag interface. So a function with flags is not a first-class value: it
can be called (directly, through method syntax, or through an alias bound
as the whole right side of a binding, `say = emit`), but not passed as an
argument, returned, stored in a list or struct, or chosen by an `if`:

    function emit declares flags (:quiet), so it can only be called
    directly or bound to another name (g = emit): it cannot be passed,
    returned or stored as a value, which would lose its flag interface

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A function with flags passed as a callback, returned, or stored in a
    list or struct of handlers.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The location is the reference that uses the function as a value.

== WHAT TO DO
Pass a small function without flags that calls it with the flags you
want, or make the option an ordinary Bool parameter if callers must
choose it through a function value.

@example reject
fn emit(x, flags :quiet):
    x

fn apply(f, x):
    f(x)

apply(emit, 1)
@caption emit is passed as an argument
@error CORE SEMANTIC FLAG-FUNCTION-VALUE
@at 7:7
@end

@example accept
fn emit(x, flags :quiet):
    if quiet:
        return 0
    x

fn emit_quietly(x):
    emit(x, :quiet)

fn apply(x, f):
    f(x)

apply(5, emit_quietly)
@caption A function without flags wraps the call
@value 0
@end

@topic error.proof-clause
title: PROOF-CLAUSE: an invalid proves clause on a function
summary: A proves PARAM: TYPE clause names no ordinary parameter, an unknown or non-refinement type, a parameter without its carrier type, or sits on a function that is neither -> bool nor -> unit.
index: errors
alias: invalid proves clause
keywords: proves, refinement, predicate, validator, refined type, carrier, -> bool, -> unit
related: concept.refinements proof.refinements error.refinement-mint-authority error.refinement-carrier warning.proves-naming
docs: REFINEMENT-VALUES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC PROOF-CLAUSE

== MEANING
A refinement (`refined type Word = str`) is a carrier type plus a fact
only the compiler tracks. Its values are minted by its owner's
functions: a predicate `fn word?(v: str) -> bool proves v: Word` (its
true result proves the fact) or a validator `fn validate_word(v: str) ->
unit proves v: Word errors Invalid` (its normal completion proves it).
The clause is checked:

    proof clause "proves w: Word" must name one of the function's
    ordinary parameters: the function has no parameter "w" (...)
        (not a flag or context parameter either);
    unknown proven type Nope in "proves v: Nope": ...
    the proven type of "proves v: Small" is int[Small], which is not a
    refinement type: ...
        (an integer domain is proven by ranges, not by proves);
    the proven parameter "v" must declare its type: ... write "v: str"
    proof carrier mismatch: parameter "v" is declared int, but Word
    refines str, ...
    a proof-producing function must declare "-> bool" (a predicate ...)
    or "-> unit" (a validator ...), but "proves v: Word" is on a function
    declared -> int

== WHY
A proof clause is a promise every caller relies on without any run-time
check: after `if word?(s):`, s is a Word. So the clause must say
precisely which parameter, which refinement and which outcome proves it.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A parameter renamed without updating its clause.
  * An untyped parameter: `fn ok?(v) -> bool proves v: Word`.
  * Proving an integer domain (`type Small = Int in ...`), which is
    entered through ranges, not through `proves`.
  * A predicate without `-> bool`, or a validator without `-> unit`.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message says which part is wrong: the parameter's name, the proven
type, the parameter's declared type, or the declared result.

== WHAT TO DO
Name an ordinary parameter, declare it of the refinement's carrier type,
prove a `refined type` (not an integer domain), and declare `-> bool` or
`-> unit`.

@example reject
refined type Word = str

fn word?(v) -> bool proves v: Word:
    true

0
@caption The proven parameter has no declared type
@error CORE SEMANTIC PROOF-CLAUSE
@at 3:28
@end

@example accept
refined type Word = str

fn word?(v: str) -> bool proves v: Word:
    v != ""

fn shout(w: Word) -> str:
    w

fn shout_if_word(s: str) -> str:
    if word?(s):
        return shout(s)
    "none"

shout_if_word("hi")
@caption A predicate proving a refinement
@value "hi"
@end

@topic error.refinement-mint-authority
title: REFINEMENT-MINT-AUTHORITY: a proof of another module's refinement
summary: A proves clause proves a refinement declared in another module (or in the entry program, from a module); only the refinement's owner may declare proofs of it.
index: errors
alias: only its owning module may mint it
keywords: refinement, proves, owner, module, authority, mint
related: concept.refinements error.proof-clause concept.modules rule.opaque-authority
docs: REFINEMENT-VALUES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC REFINEMENT-MINT-AUTHORITY

== MEANING
A refinement means what its owner's predicates and validators check:
`std::web::Emailish` is exactly what module web's predicate accepts. If any
file could declare `proves v: std::web::Emailish`, the fact would mean
nothing. So only the namespace that declares the refinement may declare
proofs of it; no import, parent or child namespace grants that
authority:

    the entry program cannot declare a proof of refinement std::web::Emailish:
    only its owning module "std::web" may mint it (no import, parent or child
    namespace grants that authority)

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A program writing its own predicate or validator for a library's
    refinement.
  * A predicate moved out of the module that declares its refinement.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names the refinement's owner; its predicates and
validators are the only way to the fact.

== WHAT TO DO
Call the owner's predicate or validator to obtain the proof
(`-help stdlib.module.std::web` lists them), or declare a refinement of your
own if you need a different fact.

@example reject
import std::web

fn mine?(v: str) -> bool proves v: std::web::Emailish:
    true

0
@caption The entry program cannot mint std::web::Emailish
@error CORE SEMANTIC REFINEMENT-MINT-AUTHORITY
@at 3:36
@end

@topic error.cyclic-refinement
title: CYCLIC-REFINEMENT: a refinement that is its own carrier
summary: A chain of refined type declarations leads back to itself; a refinement's carrier chain must end in a type that is not a refinement of it.
index: errors
alias: refinement carrier cycle
keywords: refined type, carrier, cycle, declaration
related: concept.refinements error.refinement-carrier rule.type-declaration
docs: REFINEMENT-VALUES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC CYCLIC-REFINEMENT

== MEANING
`refined type A = B` makes B the representation of A's values. A chain
`A = B`, `B = A` has no representation at all:

    cyclic refinement: "A" is its own carrier through A -> B -> A (a
    refinement's carrier chain must end in a type that is not a
    refinement of it)

It is reported at the declaration, before any code is compiled.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Two refinements, each written in terms of the other.
  * A carrier name that is a typo of another refinement's name.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message prints the whole chain of carriers.

== WHAT TO DO
End the chain in a value type: `refined type A = str`, and refine A
from B or B from A, not both.

@example reject
refined type A = B
refined type B = A

0
@caption A and B are each other's carrier
@error CORE SEMANTIC CYCLIC-REFINEMENT
@at 1:14
@end

@topic error.refinement-carrier
title: REFINEMENT-CARRIER: a refinement of a type that cannot carry one
summary: A refined type's carrier is a struct, List, function, trait or other non-scalar type; refinements are supported over str, int, bool, UnicodeChar and their domains and refinements.
index: errors
alias: invalid refinement carrier
keywords: refined type, carrier, struct, List, scalar, declaration
related: concept.refinements error.cyclic-refinement concept.integer-domains concept.opaque-structs
docs: REFINEMENT-VALUES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC REFINEMENT-CARRIER

== MEANING
A refined value has exactly its carrier's representation, with no tag
or wrapper. Today the carriers are scalar value types -- str, int, bool,
UnicodeChar, and integer-domain or refinement types over them:

    invalid refinement carrier P for "Q": P is a struct type, and
    refinements of struct values are not supported yet (...)
    invalid refinement carrier List[int] for "Q": List[int] is a stable
    value type, but refinements of aggregate, struct or callable values
    are not supported yet (...)

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Attaching a validated property to a struct, a List or a function
    value.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names the carrier and why it is not supported.

== WHAT TO DO
Refine a scalar field instead (`refined type Name = str`, used as a
struct field's type). To keep an invariant over a whole struct or list,
make it an opaque struct whose module constructs only valid values
(concept.opaque-structs).

@example reject
struct P:
    x: int

refined type Q = P

0
@caption A struct cannot carry a refinement
@error CORE SEMANTIC REFINEMENT-CARRIER
@at 4:18
@end

@topic error.binary-operand
title: BINARY-OPERAND: a binary layout operation without its static operand
summary: std::binary::emit needs a value statically of type LayoutStruct[L], std::binary::parse needs a std::abi::bytes::Bytes, and neither operation can be used as a function value.
index: errors
alias: binary emit operand; binary parse operand
keywords: binary, layout, emit, parse, LayoutStruct, Bytes, static operand
related: concept.binary-layouts error.type
docs: BINARY-LAYOUT.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC BINARY-OPERAND

== MEANING
A layout is a compile-time schema: `std::binary::parse(L, data)` imprints
bytes with layout L and `std::binary::emit(v)` writes v with the layout of
v's static type LayoutStruct[L]. The layout is read from the call at
compile time, so the operands must say it statically:

    std::binary::emit needs a value whose static type is LayoutStruct[L] for
    a layout L (the layout it emits with); this value's type here is int
    std::binary::parse imprints the bytes of a std::abi::bytes::Bytes (...); this
    argument's type here is str
    std::binary::emit can only be called directly: its layout is a static
    operand (...), so it has no function value

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Emitting a value whose static type was lost: an untyped parameter,
    a value read from a list.
  * Parsing a str or a List: std::binary::parse takes
    std::abi::bytes::Bytes.
  * `std::binary::emit` bound to a name or passed as an argument.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message shows the type the compiler sees for the operand.

== WHAT TO DO
Emit a value obtained from `std::binary::parse` (or constructed as
`LayoutStruct[L] {...}`), give `std::binary::parse` a std::abi::bytes::Bytes
(`std::abi::bytes::from_list`), and call the operations directly.

@example reject
import std::binary

std::binary::emit(5)
@caption An int has no layout
@error CORE SEMANTIC BINARY-OPERAND
@at 3:19
@end

@topic error.internal-namespace
title: INTERNAL-NAMESPACE: a namespace only the standard library may use
summary: Code outside the standard library imports, or names a member of, a namespace internal to the language implementation (table_hash, the private table hash of the standard library's hash structures).
index: errors
alias: namespace is internal to the standard library; internal namespace; private intrinsic
keywords: internal, namespace, standard library, private, table_hash, keyed hash, import
related: concept.hashable concept.imports error.internal-intrinsic-value error.missing-import
docs: HASHABLE-AND-KEYED-HASH.md IMPORTS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE INTERNAL-NAMESPACE
code: CORE SEMANTIC INTERNAL-NAMESPACE

== MEANING
A few intrinsic namespaces belong to the language implementation: their
operations exist for the standard library's own modules, which build
public facilities on them. Only a standard library module may import such a
namespace or name its members; the entry program and application modules
may not, whatever they import.

    namespace "std::table_hash" is internal to the standard library: only its
    modules may import it or name its members (the entry program is not one)

== WHAT TO DO
Use the public facility instead: `hash` for a deterministic hash
(concept.hashable). The private table hash is no API: its numbers change
from run to run and are never shown to a program.

@example reject
import std::table_hash

std::table_hash::of(1)
@caption the entry program imports the internal namespace
@error SURFACE MODULE INTERNAL-NAMESPACE
@at 1:8
@end

@topic error.internal-intrinsic-value
title: INTERNAL-INTRINSIC-VALUE: an internal intrinsic used as a value
summary: A member of an internal namespace (std::table_hash::of) may only be called, never bound, passed, returned, stored or captured as a function value.
index: errors
alias: can only be called, never used as a value; internal intrinsic value
keywords: internal, intrinsic, function value, standard library, table_hash
related: error.internal-namespace concept.hashable concept.function-types
docs: HASHABLE-AND-KEYED-HASH.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC INTERNAL-INTRINSIC-VALUE

== MEANING
Only standard library modules can reach an internal namespace
(INTERNAL-NAMESPACE), and there its members are only ever called: a
function value of one could be passed, stored or returned out of the code
allowed to use it, and a call through a value would reach it unseen. Every
use that is not the callee of a call -- an alias, an argument, a result, a
List element, a capture -- is this error. Programs outside the standard
library never see it: they cannot name the intrinsic at all.

== WHAT TO DO
Call the intrinsic directly where the value is needed, inside the standard
library module that may use it.

@topic error.missing-import
title: MISSING-IMPORT: a namespace member used without importing its namespace
summary: Code uses a member of a namespace its file does not import -- written ns::member, or reached by method syntax x.member(); add import ns to that file.
index: errors
alias: namespace is not imported; add import; not imported
keywords: import, namespace, qualified, method syntax, method sugar, exact imports, transitive, static compile, compiler
related: concept.imports rule.method-requires-import rule.imports-not-transitive concept.method-sugar error.unknown-namespace error.unknown-symbol principle.explicit-dependencies
docs: IMPORTS.md DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md METHOD-SUGAR.md
code: SURFACE MODULE MISSING-IMPORT
code: CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-IMPORT

== MEANING
A file may use the members of another namespace only after importing
that namespace itself (`import std::str`). Imports are per file, exact and
not transitive: importing `abi` does not import `std::abi::bytes`, and a
module's imports do nothing for the files that import the module. The
member exists -- otherwise the diagnostic would be UNKNOWN-SYMBOL or
UNKNOWN-NAMESPACE -- but this file is not authorized to name it.

It is reported for each use, in both spellings:

  * Qualified, `std::str::length(s)`: the module loader reports it before
    anything else is compiled, as SURFACE MODULE MISSING-IMPORT:
        namespace "std::str" is not imported; add `import std::str` to use
        `std::str::length`
    with a note when a related namespace is imported:
        ... (imports are exact: `import std::abi` does not import
        "std::abi::bytes")
  * Method syntax, `s.length()`: method syntax applies only functions
    visible by name, and an unimported namespace's members are not
    visible. When no visible function fits but a function of that name
    in an unimported namespace would accept the receiver, the call is
    reported as the missing import, CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-IMPORT, at the
    method name:
        namespace "std::str" is not imported; add `import std::str` to use
        `std::str::length` (the method-style call ".length(...)" applies only
        a function visible by that name)
    In an untyped function, the method's receiver type is known only
    for a call, so the problem is reported at the call:
        call to shout with s : str is invalid: namespace "std::str" is not
        imported; ... (in the body of shout, at 2:5)
  * Also: a qualified enum case of an unimported module
    (`std::compiler::DiagnosticCode::Unbound`), and `static compile:` in a
    file without `import std::compiler` (a rejected block fails with
    std::compiler::CompilerError).

Earlier compilers reported the method-syntax case as a confusing TYPE
error elsewhere ("function result does not prove declared type str");
since the diagnostic recovery work (DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md) it is always
this diagnostic.

== WHY
What a file can name is decided by its own header, never by what other
files import or what happens to be loaded: adding an import to one file
never changes what another file means. Method syntax does no search by
receiver type, for the same reason.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Using a standard-library function without its import (`str`, `list`,
    `mutable_vector` ...; the root primitives such as `mod` and `argv`
    need none).
  * Expecting an import to cover a child namespace (`import std::linux` for
    `std::linux::io`) or to flow from an imported module.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names the namespace to import and the member. Check the
header of the file the location is in: in a program of several files it
may be a module, which needs imports of its own.

== WHAT TO DO
Add `import NS` (the message names it) to the header of the file that
uses the member. `-help stdlib` lists the standard namespaces and their
members.

@example reject
std::str::length("abc")
@caption A qualified use of str without import std::str
@error SURFACE MODULE MISSING-IMPORT
@at 1:1
@end

@example reject
import std::abi

std::abi::bytes::from_list([])
@caption abi does not import std::abi::bytes
@error SURFACE MODULE MISSING-IMPORT
@at 3:1
@end

@example reject
fn greet(name: str) -> str:
    name.concat(" says hi")

greet("Ada")
@caption Method syntax without import std::str
@error CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-IMPORT
@at 2:10
@end

@example accept
import std::str

"abc".length()
@caption With import std::str, method syntax works too
@value 3
@end

@topic error.unknown-namespace
title: UNKNOWN-NAMESPACE: a namespace that does not exist
summary: No standard namespace, application module or set of intrinsics has this name: there is no ROOT/NAME.bot beside the entry program and no stdlib/NAME.bot.
index: errors
alias: unknown namespace; no such module; module not found
keywords: import, namespace, module, application module, standard library, path, file beside, nested namespace
related: concept.imports concept.modules rule.application-modules error.missing-import error.unknown-symbol error.module-collision error.not-an-enum
docs: STD-NAMESPACE.md IMPORTS.md STDLIB-NAMESPACES.md
code: SURFACE MODULE UNKNOWN-NAMESPACE

== MEANING
`import NAME` (and a qualified `NAME::member`) names a namespace, decided
by its first segment, with no search and no fallback:

  1. `std::NAME`: the standard library's module `NAME.bot` (`a/b.bot` for
     `std::a::b`) in stdlib/, or compiler intrinsics of that name
     (`std::str`, `std::char` have no module file);
  2. any other NAME, for the entry program and its application modules:
     the application module `NAME.bot` in the entry file's directory (the
     application root; `a/b.bot` for `a::b`).

Nothing else is searched: not the working directory, not an environment
variable, not a parent directory, and a name without its std:: is never
looked up in the standard library. A standard-library module's own
imports see only the standard library. When the namespace does not exist,
the message names where it looked, and the standard namespace of the same
name if there is one:

    unknown namespace "std::list": no such module file ROOT/list.bot (an
    application's own modules are the files beside its entry program);
    the standard library's namespace of that name is std::list (`import
    std::list`): standard namespaces are named from the root std, and a
    namespace outside it is never looked up there

`std` itself is the standard library's root, not a namespace (`import
std` is rejected the same way).

A qualified name `X::Y` whose X is neither a namespace nor an enum type
of this file is reported the same way, with "(nor is "X" an enum type
visible in this file: a case is written Enum::Case)".

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A standard namespace written without its std:: (`import std::list` for
    `import std::list`): the message names the standard one.
  * A typo, or a name from another language's library (`math`, `os`).
  * An application module that is not beside the entry file: the root
    is the entry file's directory, whatever the working directory, and a
    module in a subdirectory `util/` is the namespace `util::NAME`.
  * A parent of a namespace imported as if it were one: `import
    std::linux::abi` is not a namespace; `std::linux::abi::x86_64` is.
  * A namespace name with a member appended: `std::list::length::x`.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message prints both places searched. Check that the module file
exists at the first one, spelled exactly (letter case, `a/b.bot` for
`a::b`), beside the entry file actually compiled.

== WHAT TO DO
Fix the name (a standard namespace is std::NAME), or move the module to
`NAME.bot` beside the entry program. `-help stdlib` lists the standard
namespaces.

@example reject
import nosuch

0
@caption No module or intrinsics named nosuch
@error SURFACE MODULE UNKNOWN-NAMESPACE
@at 1:8
@end

@topic error.unknown-symbol
title: UNKNOWN-SYMBOL: an imported namespace has no member of that name
summary: The namespace is imported (or is the file's own), but it has no definition, type, struct or error of the written name.
index: errors
alias: namespace has no definition; no such member
keywords: member, definition, function, type, struct, error, intrinsic, namespace, typo
related: concept.imports error.missing-import error.unknown-namespace error.not-a-type concept.modules
docs: IMPORTS.md STDLIB-NAMESPACES.md
code: SURFACE MODULE UNKNOWN-SYMBOL

== MEANING
After `import NS`, a qualified reference `NS::name` must name something
NS declares, of the right kind for where it is written. The message
lists what NS has:

    namespace "std::list" has no definition "nosuch" (it defines: all?, any?,
    append, at, find, get, length, none?)
    namespace "std::str" has no definition "reverse" (its members are the
    intrinsics: append_char, char_at, concat, ...)
    namespace "std::test" has no type "Nope" (it declares: Erased, Match, ...)
    namespace "std::list" has no error "Nope" (it declares: NotFound)
    namespace "std::list" has no type "Foo" (its types: none)  (import type)

and `std::ffi::c::Bool` or `std::ffi::c::Ptr` used as a value ("is a type,
not a value or a function").

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A function another language's library has (`std::str::reverse`,
    `std::list::map`); Botlish's standard library is small, and absent
    facilities have capability.* pages.
  * A type or error spelled with the wrong namespace.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message lists the namespace's members of the kind written
(definitions, types, errors).

== WHAT TO DO
Use a member the message lists (`-help stdlib.module.NS` describes
them), or write the function yourself.

@example reject
import std::list

std::list::nosuch([1])
@caption list has no definition nosuch
@error SURFACE MODULE UNKNOWN-SYMBOL
@at 3:1
@end

@topic error.not-a-type
title: NOT-A-TYPE: import type of a member that is not a type declaration
summary: import type NS::T names a struct, a function, an error, a layout or an intrinsic type; import type imports type declarations, enums and traits only.
index: errors
alias: is a struct, not a source-defined type
keywords: import type, struct, type declaration, enum, function, error, layout, qualified
related: rule.import-type concept.imports capability.struct-type-import concept.structs concept.enums error.type-import-collision
docs: IMPORTS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE NOT-A-TYPE

== MEANING
`import type NS::T` binds the short name T in this file for a type that
module NS declares with `type`, `refined type`, `enum` or `trait`. It
does not import structs: a struct of another module is always spelled
qualified, `NS::Name`, after `import NS`. Other members are not types:

    "std::test::Suite" is a struct, not a source-defined `type`: `import type`
    imports `type` declarations only; spell the struct std::test::Suite after
    `import std::test`
    "std::list::get" exists, but is not a type (it is a function or value of
    namespace "std::list"): ...
    "std::compiler::CompilerError" is an error declaration, not a type
    "std::binary::X" is a layout, not a type: ... (its values have the type
    LayoutStruct[std::binary::X])
    "std::ffi::c::Ptr" is an intrinsic type of namespace "std::ffi::c", not a
    source-defined `type`: spell it std::ffi::c::Ptr after `import std::ffi::c`

The struct case is the one people meet most: `import type` works for an
enum of a module but not for a struct of the same module, and there is
no other way to give a module's struct a short name (structs keep their
qualified spelling, capability.struct-type-import).

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Using `import type` as the way to import any named member: structs,
    functions, errors.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message says what the member is instead.

== WHAT TO DO
For a struct, replace `import type NS::Name` by `import NS` and write
`NS::Name` in annotations and constructions. For a function or error,
import the namespace and use it qualified:

    import std::compiler

    fn line_of(at: std::compiler::SourceLocation) -> int:
        at.line

@example reject
import type std::test::Suite

0
@caption import type does not import structs
@error SURFACE MODULE NOT-A-TYPE
@at 1:24
@end

@example reject
import type std::list::get

0
@caption std::list::get is a function, not a type
@error SURFACE MODULE NOT-A-TYPE
@at 1:24
@end

@topic error.type-import-collision
title: TYPE-IMPORT-COLLISION: import type binds a type name that is already taken
summary: The short name an import type binds is also a type this file declares, a built-in type, or the short name of another import type.
index: errors
alias: type import collision
keywords: import type, collision, short name, shadow, built-in type, qualified
related: concept.imports error.not-a-type error.duplicate-import
docs: IMPORTS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE TYPE-IMPORT-COLLISION

== MEANING
A type import's short name must denote exactly one type in the file. It
never shadows, and is never shadowed by, a type the file declares, a
built-in type or another import -- there is no precedence and no
renaming:

    `import type std::compiler::DiagnosticCode` binds the type name
    "DiagnosticCode", which this file also declares itself (at ...); an
    imported type never shadows or is shadowed by a local one: drop the
    import and spell the imported type std::compiler::DiagnosticCode (after
    `import std::compiler`)
    `import type std::ffi::c::Int` binds the type name "Int", which is a
    built-in type; ...
    `import type std::abi::aarch64::Register64Word` and `import type
    std::abi::x86_64::Register64Word` (at ...) both bind the type name
    "Register64Word"; ...

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A type both imported and declared locally, after a refactoring.
  * Two modules with types of the same short name.
  * `import type std::ffi::c::Int` (`Int` is a built-in name).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names both sides and the location of the other one.

== WHAT TO DO
Drop the `import type` and spell the type qualified after `import NS`
(or rename your own type).

@example reject
import type std::compiler::DiagnosticCode

enum DiagnosticCode:
    A,

0
@caption The file declares its own DiagnosticCode
@error SURFACE MODULE TYPE-IMPORT-COLLISION
@at 1:28
@end

@topic error.duplicate-import
title: DUPLICATE-IMPORT: the same namespace or type imported twice
summary: A file's header imports the same namespace, or the same type, more than once.
index: errors
alias: already imported
keywords: import, import type, header, duplicate
related: concept.imports error.type-import-collision error.self-import
docs: IMPORTS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE DUPLICATE-IMPORT

== MEANING
Each import states one dependency of the file, once:

    namespace "std::list" is already imported (first imported at ...)
    type "std::byte::Byte" is already imported (first imported at ...)

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Two headers merged, or an import added twice.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message gives the location of the first import.

== WHAT TO DO
Delete the second import.

@example reject
import std::list
import std::list

0
@caption list is imported twice
@error SURFACE MODULE DUPLICATE-IMPORT
@at 2:8
@end

@topic error.self-import
title: SELF-IMPORT: a file that imports itself or its own namespace
summary: The entry program imports its own file, or a module imports its own namespace or one of its own types; a module needs no import for its own members.
index: errors
alias: import own namespace; entry program imports itself
keywords: import, entry program, module, own namespace, self
related: concept.imports concept.modules error.cycle error.duplicate-import
docs: IMPORTS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE SELF-IMPORT

== MEANING
A module is inside its namespace: it names its own members bare and
needs no import of them. The entry program is in no namespace and is not
a module, so it can be imported by nobody, itself included:

    "game" names this program's own entry file (DIR/game.bot): an entry
    program is in no namespace and is not a module, so it cannot be
    imported
    a file of namespace "helper" cannot import its own namespace: a
    module is inside its namespace and needs no import to name its own
    members
    a file of namespace "geo" cannot import a type of its own namespace:
    its own type "Point" is already usable by that name

An entry file named like a standard namespace (`test.bot` importing
`test`) imports the standard one: that is no self-import. A module of
the program that imports the entry file is a CYCLE.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * The entry program named like the module it was meant to import
    (`game.bot` importing a `game` module).
  * A module that imports its own namespace to qualify its own names.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Compare the file names of the program's directory with its imports.

== WHAT TO DO
Delete the import. To share code between programs, move it into a
module file beside them and import that.

@example reject
import example

0
@caption Saved as example.bot, it imports itself
@error SURFACE MODULE SELF-IMPORT
@at 1:8
@end

@topic error.cycle
title: CYCLE: modules that import each other
summary: A chain of imports leads back to a module being loaded (a -> b -> a), or a module imports the entry program; module dependencies must be acyclic.
index: errors
alias: module dependency cycle; circular import
keywords: import, cycle, circular, module, dependency, entry program
related: concept.modules concept.imports error.self-import rule.application-modules
docs: IMPORTS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE CYCLE

== MEANING
A module is loaded once, after everything it imports. Two modules that
import each other, directly or through others, have no such order:

    module dependency cycle: a -> b -> a
    module dependency cycle: DIR/main.bot (the entry program) -> helper
    -> main (an entry program is not a module and cannot be imported)

It is reported at the import that closes the cycle. A cycle needs at
least one module file, so there is no single-file example here; the
first message comes from three files in one directory:

    main.bot              a.bot                 b.bot
      import a              import b              import a

      a::f()                fn f() -> int:        fn g() -> int:
                                1                     2

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Two modules that each need a helper of the other.
  * A module that imports the entry program to reach a function defined
    there.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message prints the chain of imports; the location is the import
that closes it.

== WHAT TO DO
Move what both modules need into a third module that both import, or
merge the two. A module must never import the entry program: move the
shared code from the entry program into a module.

@topic error.module-collision
title: MODULE-COLLISION: an application module named like an intrinsic namespace
summary: An application file imports a namespace that is both a file beside the entry program and a namespace of compiler intrinsics registered outside std; neither is chosen silently.
index: errors
alias: application module has the name of an intrinsic namespace
keywords: application module, intrinsic namespace, collision, rename, test fixture, entry file directory
related: rule.application-modules concept.modules concept.imports concept.standard-library error.unknown-namespace error.reserved-namespace
docs: STD-NAMESPACE.md IMPORTS.md POST-2048-PRODUCT-FIXES.md
code: SURFACE MODULE MODULE-COLLISION

== MEANING
Every standard namespace is under std (concept.standard-library), so an
application module `list.bot` and the standard `std::list` never collide:
they are two namespaces. A collision is left only where one name has two
meanings outside std: an application file NAME.bot and compiler intrinsics
of the namespace NAME, which only a test process registers (its
`ffi_fixture` fixture). The import is rejected, naming the file:

    application module DIR/ffi_fixture.bot has the name of the intrinsic
    namespace "ffi_fixture" (compiler intrinsics registered in this
    process), which `import ffi_fixture` names: an intrinsic namespace is
    never replaced by an application file, and never silently chosen over
    one; rename the application module

An application file that nothing imports collides with nothing. A
collision needs a second file and a test process, so there is no
single-file example.

== WHAT TO DO
Rename the application module and its imports.

@topic error.reserved-namespace
title: RESERVED-NAMESPACE: an application file inside std/
summary: An application file imports std::NAME while the application has a file std/NAME.bot beside its entry program: std belongs to the standard library, so that file is never a module and is never chosen.
index: errors
alias: application module named std; shadow the standard library; std directory in my project
keywords: std, standard library, reserved, shadow, application module, std/ directory, rename
related: concept.standard-library rule.application-modules concept.modules error.unknown-namespace error.module-collision
docs: STD-NAMESPACE.md IMPORTS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE RESERVED-NAMESPACE

== MEANING
The namespace std and every namespace under it are the standard library's
(concept.standard-library): `std::list` is stdlib/list.bot beside the
compiler, for every program. An application file ROOT/std/list.bot would
be the namespace std::list by its path, so it can never be an application
module, and `import std::list` never chooses between the two. Rather than
ignore the file silently, the import is rejected, naming it:

    application file DIR/std/list.bot would be the namespace "std::list",
    but std and every namespace under it belong to the standard library:
    an application module is never a standard namespace, and `import
    std::list` never chooses between the two (it names the standard
    library's LIB/list.bot); move or rename the application file out of
    the application's std/ directory

The same holds for a file under std/ for a namespace the standard library
does not have (`import std::mine` beside std/mine.bot). An application
file under std/ that nothing imports as std::NAME collides with nothing.
A shadowing attempt needs a second file, so there is no single-file
example.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the application's module out of its std/ directory (std/util.bot to
util.bot, imported as `util`) or rename the directory.

@topic error.rooted-name
title: ROOTED-NAME: a rooted name with no namespace
summary: A leading :: starts a namespace path from the root (::std::list::at); a name in no namespace, such as the entry program's own function or type, is written without it.
index: errors
alias: leading ::; absolute name; ::name
keywords: ::, rooted, absolute, root namespace, qualified name, std, syntax
related: concept.standard-library concept.imports error.surface-syntax error.unknown-namespace
docs: STD-NAMESPACE.md
code: ROOTED-NAME
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | ROOTED-NAME: *

== MEANING
`::` at the start of a name roots its namespace path explicitly, as in
Tcl: `import ::std::list`, `::std::list::at(xs, 0)`, `x:
::std::abi::U8Value`, `on ::std::list::NotFound:`. A rooted reference is
a namespace path plus a member, so it has at least two segments after
the root. One segment alone names nothing in a namespace -- the entry
program's own definitions, types and errors are in no namespace -- and
the parser says so:

    "::f" is a rooted name with no namespace: a leading "::" starts a
    namespace path from the root, so a name written with it is
    ::NAMESPACE::NAME (::std::list::at); a name that is in no namespace
    (the entry program's own) is written without it: f

(An import's path is a namespace, so `import ::game` is the application
module game.)

== WHAT TO DO
Drop the leading `::`, or name the member's namespace after it.

@example reject
fn twice(x):
    x * 2

::twice(4)
@caption The entry program's own function is in no namespace
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 4:1
@end

@example accept
import ::std::list

[::std::list::length([1, 2]), std::list::length([3])]
@caption Rooted and unrooted spellings of one standard namespace
@value [2, 1]
@end

@topic error.invalid-toplevel
title: INVALID-TOPLEVEL: a statement at the top level of a module
summary: A module file holds a top-level expression, call, loop or destructuring; a module's top level holds only declarations and immutable bindings.
index: errors
alias: statement at module top level
keywords: module, top level, statement, expression, call, destructuring, declarations
related: concept.modules rule.module-bindings error.module-immutable concept.program-value
docs: MODULE-BINDINGS.md IMPORTS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE INVALID-TOPLEVEL

== MEANING
Only the entry program runs statements and has a value. A module is a
set of definitions: functions, immutable bindings and type, struct,
enum, layout, trait, error and extern declarations. Anything else at
its top level is rejected when the module is loaded:

    module "helper" (DIR/helper.bot): only function definitions,
    immutable bindings, type declarations, ... are allowed at module top
    level, found a "call" statement
    module "helper" (...): a struct destructuring is not allowed at
    module top level (module bindings are context-free immutable values,
    and a struct value is not one: MODULE-BINDINGS.md); destructure
    inside a function

(`with context` at a module's top level is reported as
CONTEXT-INSTALLATION-UNSUPPORTED.)
It needs a module file, so there is no single-file example:

    main.bot                    helper.bot
      import helper               fn f() -> int:
                                      1
      helper::f()
                                  f()        <- rejected

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A test call or a print left at a module's top level.
  * Code moved from the entry program into a module unchanged.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names the module, its file and the kind of statement.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the statement into the entry program, or into a function of the
module that the entry program calls. A module binding (`limit = 3`) is
fine; a test call is not.

@topic error.duplicate-native
title: DUPLICATE-NATIVE: a library module redefining a compiler intrinsic
summary: A standard-library module defines a member whose qualified name is a compiler-provided intrinsic (such as std::list::at); an intrinsic cannot be redefined or overloaded.
index: errors
alias: cannot redefine an intrinsic
keywords: intrinsic, native, module, redefine, overload, standard library
related: concept.modules stdlib.module.std::list error.module-collision
docs: STDLIB-NAMESPACES.md IMPORTS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE DUPLICATE-NATIVE

== MEANING
Some members of standard namespaces are intrinsics provided by the
compiler (`std::list::at`, `std::str::concat`). Every reference spelled
`std::list::at` denotes the intrinsic, so a module definition of that name
could never be called, and Botlish has no overloading for it to coexist
as:

    module "std::list" (LIB/list.bot) cannot define "at": std::list::at is a
    compiler-provided intrinsic (...), which every reference of that
    spelling denotes; it cannot be redefined or overloaded (other members
    of namespace "std::list" are unaffected)

Only a standard-library module can be in such a namespace: an
application module named like a standard namespace is a MODULE-COLLISION
first, and the entry program's definitions are plain names. So this
diagnostic is met only when editing the standard library itself, and
there is no example program for it.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A library change that adds a function named like an intrinsic of the
    same namespace.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
`-help NS::NAME` describes the intrinsic the definition collides
with.

== WHAT TO DO
Give the library function another name; the module may define any other
member of its namespace (stdlib/list.bot's `std::list::get` beside the
intrinsic `std::list::at`).

@topic error.module-immutable
title: MODULE-IMMUTABLE: a module binding whose value is not provably immutable
summary: A module-level binding retains a value the compiler cannot prove transitively immutable, or reads a later module binding; module bindings are immutable, context-free values.
index: errors
alias: module binding cannot retain mutable value
keywords: module binding, global, constant, immutable, mutable, MutableArray, initializer, order
related: rule.module-bindings concept.modules concept.values error.invalid-toplevel
docs: MODULE-BINDINGS.md MODULE-STATIC-RETAINED-VALUES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC MODULE-IMMUTABLE

== MEANING
A module binding (`hex_digits = [...]` at a module's top level) is
evaluated once and shared by every caller, so it must be a value nobody
can change: the compiler proves it transitively immutable (Ints, Bools,
Strings, Lists of such, functions capturing such) and context-free
(MODULE-CONTEXT, checked first: creating a MutableArray or a
MutableVector is not context-free, so such an initializer is reported
there). This check rejects:

    module binding "NAME" cannot retain mutable value ...
    module binding initializer may refer only to earlier module
    bindings: ...
    module binding "cfg::limit" cannot prove its retained value is
    transitively immutable: control-flow result cannot be proven
    immutable

The proof is deliberately small and conservative: it follows literals,
List construction and direct calls of functions whose bodies it can
prove, and gives up on an unknown shape -- a call of a function whose
body runs a `loop:` is rejected even when it returns an Int. This
applies to modules only; the entry program's top-level bindings are
ordinary bindings. It needs a module file, so there is no single-file
example:

    cfg.bot:  fn compute() -> int:
                  loop:
                      break
                  3

              limit = compute()       <- rejected

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A module-level "constant" computed by a helper function the proof
    cannot follow (one with a loop).
  * A module binding meant to hold shared mutable state.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message ends with the part of the value the proof could not
follow.

== WHAT TO DO
Write the value directly (`limit = 3`) or with expressions the proof
follows, make it a function the callers call (`fn limit() -> int`), or
build mutable state inside the functions that use it.

@topic error.ambiguous-module
title: AMBIGUOUS-MODULE: a namespace both general and architecture-specific
summary: A standard-library namespace has both a module file NAME.bot and architecture-specific files NAME.ARCH.bot; a namespace is one or the other.
index: errors
alias: module file and architecture-specific module files
keywords: architecture, module file, x86_64, aarch64, standard library, std::linux::sys
related: error.unsupported-architecture error.unknown-target backend.targets concept.modules
docs: LINUX-SYSCALL-TRANSPORTS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE AMBIGUOUS-MODULE

== MEANING
A standard namespace is either the same on every architecture (one file
`stdlib/NAME.bot`) or specific to each (`stdlib/NAME.x86_64.bot`,
`stdlib/NAME.aarch64.bot`, chosen by the compilation's target, like
`std::linux::sys`), never both:

    namespace "std::linux::sys" has both a module file ... and
    architecture-specific module files (...): a namespace is either the
    same on every architecture or specific to each, never both

Only the standard library has architecture-specific files (application
modules are always one file), and the shipped library has no such
namespace: the diagnostic means the library installation was edited or
mixed. There is no example program for it.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Library files of another compiler version copied into the library
    directory.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names every file of the namespace.

== WHAT TO DO
Remove the stray file from the library directory, or restore the library
of this compiler version.

@topic error.unknown-target
title: UNKNOWN-TARGET: a target-dependent namespace on an unknown host
summary: The program imports an architecture-specific namespace or std::ffi::c, and the host machine is none of the known C ABI targets; compile with -target.
index: errors
alias: none of the known targets
keywords: target, -target, host, architecture, std::ffi::c, std::linux::sys, C ABI
related: backend.targets backend.platforms cli.options error.unsupported-architecture error.ambiguous-module concept.c-ffi
docs: C-ABI-TYPES.md LINUX-SYSCALL-TRANSPORTS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE UNKNOWN-TARGET

== MEANING
Two kinds of namespace depend on the compilation's C ABI target, which
defaults to the host's: an architecture-specific module (`std::linux::sys`)
and `std::ffi::c`, whose integer types have the target's sizes. On a host
that is none of the known targets (linux, macos and windows on x86_64 and
aarch64), the compiler cannot choose:

    namespace "std::linux::sys" is architecture-specific (its files are ...),
    and this host (OS MACHINE) is none of the known targets; compile with
    -target (linux-aarch64, linux-x86_64, macos-aarch64, ...)
    namespace "std::ffi::c" needs a C ABI target, and this host (...) is none
    of the known ones; compile with -target (...)

A platform namespace (`linux` and everything under it:
error.unsupported-platform) needs a target too, for its operating system:

    namespace "std::linux::io" belongs to the Linux platform, and this host
    (...) is none of the known targets; compile with -target (...)

It depends on the host, so there is no example program for it.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Compiling on a host other than Linux, macOS or Windows on x86_64 or
    aarch64 (another operating system, or another processor).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message prints the host's operating system and machine.

== WHAT TO DO
Pass the target explicitly: `botlish -target linux-x86_64 PROGRAM.bot`.

@topic error.unsupported-platform
title: UNSUPPORTED-PLATFORM: a platform's namespace imported for another system
summary: A platform's namespace (linux::*, darwin::* or windows::*) is imported for a target of another operating system; compile for that platform or drop the import.
index: errors
alias: Linux platform; linux namespace on macOS; darwin namespace on Linux; macOS platform; Windows platform; windows namespace on Linux
keywords: platform, linux, std::linux::io, std::linux::sys, std::linux::path, darwin, std::darwin::io, std::darwin::path, windows, std::windows::io, macOS, Windows, target, -target, operating system
related: error.unknown-target error.unsupported-architecture backend.platforms backend.targets cli.options concept.modules
docs: MACOS-AARCH64-MILESTONE-2.md MACOS-AARCH64-MILESTONE-8.md WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-2.md LINUX-SYSCALL-TRANSPORTS.md C-ABI-TYPES.md
code: SURFACE MODULE UNSUPPORTED-PLATFORM

== MEANING
A compilation target is an operating system and an architecture
(backend.targets). The standard namespace `linux` and every namespace under
it (`std::linux::io`, `std::linux::path`, `std::linux::sys`, the system-call
transports) belong to the Linux platform: they make Linux system calls or
model Linux, so they exist for Linux targets only. Imported for a target of
another operating system, they are refused at the import:

    namespace "std::linux::io" belongs to the Linux platform (the standard
    namespace linux and every namespace under it), which is unavailable
    for target macos-aarch64 (macOS on aarch64): a Linux module is never
    chosen for, mapped to or substituted on another operating system,
    whatever the architecture; compile for a Linux target (-target
    linux-aarch64) or import no Linux namespace

The macOS platform's namespaces are refused the same way for every Linux and
Windows target: `darwin` and every namespace under it (`std::darwin::io`,
`std::darwin::path`), which call macOS's libSystem, name the root `darwin`
in the message; and the Windows platform's for every Linux and macOS target:
`windows` and `std::windows::io`, which call kernel32, name the root
`windows` (both Windows targets, MSVC's and MinGW's, have it). Which
namespace trees are a platform's is the target table's designation
(core/cabi.tcl's platforms), never a namespace's spelling.

A matching architecture is not enough: linux-aarch64 and macos-aarch64
share an instruction set, not an operating system, and no macOS module
stands in for a Linux one, nor the reverse. Namespaces that are no
platform's stay available on every target, the architecture-specific
ones included (`std::abi::aarch64` on macOS). The target defaults to the
host's, so the same program compiles on Linux and is refused on a Mac;
there is no example program for it.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * Compiling a program that prints through `std::linux::io` (or calls
    `linux` directly) on macOS or Windows, or with -target naming one.
  * Compiling a program that prints through `std::darwin::io` on Linux or
    Windows (examples/io/hello-darwin.bot outside a Mac).
  * Compiling a program that prints through `std::windows::io` on Linux or
    macOS (examples/io/hello-windows.bot outside Windows).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names the namespace and the target, with its operating
system and architecture. `-target` on the command line overrides the
host's default.

== WHAT TO DO
Compile for the platform's target (`botlish -target linux-aarch64
PROGRAM.bot`, `-target macos-aarch64`): the Tcl backends check the result on
any host, while native code runs only on its own target's host (NATIVE
TARGET). Or install the host's own context instead: a function printing
through the portable io needs no change, only the entry program's import and
`with context` (std::linux::io on Linux, std::darwin::io on macOS,
std::windows::io on Windows). The portable standard library (io,
std::io::path) has no platform.

@topic error.unsupported-architecture
title: UNSUPPORTED-ARCHITECTURE: no module file for the target's architecture
summary: An architecture-specific namespace has no file for the architecture of the compilation's target.
index: errors
alias: no module for the architecture
keywords: architecture, target, module file, x86_64, aarch64, -target
related: error.unknown-target error.ambiguous-module backend.targets backend.platforms
docs: LINUX-SYSCALL-TRANSPORTS.md
code: SURFACE MODULE UNSUPPORTED-ARCHITECTURE

== MEANING
An architecture-specific standard namespace is a set of files
`stdlib/NAME.ARCH.bot`, one per supported architecture. When the target's
architecture has none:

    namespace "NAME" has no module for the ARCH architecture of target
    TARGET: it is architecture-specific, and its files are ... (no
    stdlib/NAME.ARCH.bot)

Every architecture-specific namespace of the shipped library
(`std::linux::sys`) has a file for both x86_64 and aarch64, the
architectures of all known targets, so a program compiled against the
shipped library does not meet this diagnostic; there is no example
program for it.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * A library extended with an architecture-specific namespace for only
    some architectures.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names the namespace's files and the missing one.

== WHAT TO DO
Compile for a target whose architecture the namespace supports, or add
the missing architecture's module file to the library.

@topic error.refinement-parameter
title: REFINEMENT-PARAMETER: a scalar refinement with wrong parameters
summary: A type NAME{PARAM: N, ...} names no refinement family, or misses a required parameter, names an unknown one, or gives values out of range or contradicting each other.
index: errors
alias: refinement parameter; scalar refinement parameter; minDecimalsPreserved missing; maxDecimalsPreserved below minDecimalsPreserved; bare DecimalRounded; negative list length
keywords: DecimalRounded, minDecimalsPreserved, maxDecimalsPreserved, List, length, braces, parameter, refinement, annotation, type
related: concept.scalar-refinements concept.decimal-rounded concept.list-length rule.type-annotation error.type error.decimal-precision
docs: DECIMAL-ROUNDED-MILESTONE-1.md LIST-CARDINALITY-REFINEMENTS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC REFINEMENT-PARAMETER

== MEANING
A scalar refinement is a type of a compiler-known family with integer
parameters, written in braces after the type it refines; the parameters
are part of the type (concept.scalar-refinements). The families:
DecimalRounded (concept.decimal-rounded): minDecimalsPreserved is
required, maxDecimalsPreserved optional, both from 0 to 1000, and an upper
bound is at least the guaranteed precision; and List[T]
(concept.list-length): length is required, a non-negative integer. The
message names every problem of the annotation:

    DecimalRounded requires "minDecimalsPreserved" (write
    DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: N})
    an upper precision bound below the guaranteed precision describes no
    value: maxDecimalsPreserved must be at least minDecimalsPreserved
    DecimalRounded's "minDecimalsPreserved" must be from 0 to 1000, not 1001
    "decimals" is not a parameter of DecimalRounded (its parameters: ...)
    DecimalRounded is written with its refinement parameters, which are
    part of the type: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: N}
    List[T]'s "length" must be at least 0, not -1
    "size" is not a parameter of List[T] (its parameters: length)
    "Float64" has no scalar refinements: NAME{...} refines one of
    DecimalRounded, List[T]

A malformed field (no colon, a value that is not an integer literal, a
field written twice) is a syntax error where it is written instead.

== WHAT TO DO
Write the parameters the family requires, with values in range:
DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}, or with a ceiling
DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2, maxDecimalsPreserved: 4};
List[int]{length: 4}. A bare DecimalRounded has no precision and is no
annotation.

@example reject
fn price(x: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 4, maxDecimalsPreserved: 2}) -> int:
    1

price
@caption A ceiling below the guarantee describes no value
@error CORE SEMANTIC REFINEMENT-PARAMETER
@at 1:9
@end

@example reject
fn price() -> DecimalRounded:
    1.5

price()
@caption The parameters are part of the type
@error CORE SEMANTIC REFINEMENT-PARAMETER
@at 1:9
@end

@example accept
fn price() -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2, maxDecimalsPreserved: 4}:
    1.505

price()
@value 1.50
@end

@topic error.decimal-precision
title: DECIMAL-PRECISION: a decimal constant not proven to the places required
summary: A decimal constant (a literal, or + - * / of literals) where DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: N} is required, whose run is not proven to round correctly at N places.
index: errors
alias: only proven correct to; decimal places not proven; precision loss; not proven correct; divides by an exactly zero decimal
keywords: DecimalRounded, decimal, places, precision, proof, binary64, literal, constant, rounding, cancellation, large number
related: concept.decimal-rounded error.decimal-guarantee-lost error.refinement-parameter error.known-error concept.floats
docs: DECIMAL-ROUNDED-MILESTONE-1.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DECIMAL-PRECISION

== MEANING
The compiler proves every DecimalRounded constant: it computes the exact
decimal result and, with exact arithmetic, the binary64 a run computes
(each literal's chosen representation, then each operation correctly
rounded), and checks that the run's value rounds, half to even, to the
exact result's decimal at every place up to N. When it does not:

    this result is only proven correct to 16 decimal places, but the
    declared result of third requires DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved:
    17} (its exact value is 1/3 = 0.33333333333333333333...; its binary64
    computation gives 0.3333333333333333148296162562473909929394..., which
    rounds to 0.33333333333333331 at 17 places where the exact value
    rounds to 0.33333333333333333)

"no decimal place ... is proven correct (not even its rounding to an
integer)" means not even round(t, 0) holds: binary64 does not hold the
integer digits (1.0e23 is 99999999999999991611392 in binary64). A quotient
whose divisor's exact decimal is zero, though its binary64 is not (0.1 *
3.0 - 0.3 is 5.55e-17), has no value to round, and is reported the same
way. A binary64 result that is not finite is NonFiniteFloat instead
(KNOWN-ERROR for a constant): a different event.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * More places than binary64 holds at the magnitude: about 15 to 17
    significant digits in all, so 1.0e20 + 0.01 holds one place.
  * Cancellation: a difference of nearly equal values keeps the error of
    its operands (0.1 * 3.0 - 0.3 holds 15 places, not 16).
  * A tie that binary64 cannot place on the right side at that precision.

== WHAT TO DO
Require the places the program needs (the guarantee is a minimum: a
weaker type accepts the same constant), or write the constant so that it
holds them (one literal instead of a sum). The message's exact value and
binary64 value show where the digits part.

@example reject
fn big() -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}:
    1.0e20 + 0.01

big()
@caption Binary64 holds 1.0e20 + 0.01 to one place
@error CORE SEMANTIC DECIMAL-PRECISION
@at 2:5
@end

@example accept
fn big() -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 1}:
    1.0e20 + 0.01

big()
@value 100000000000000000000.0
@end

@topic error.decimal-guarantee-lost
title: DECIMAL-GUARANTEE-LOST: DecimalRounded arithmetic that proves nothing
summary: + - * / of DecimalRounded values that are not constants (parameters, call results) proves no decimal place, so it is rejected; decay to Float64 to compute.
index: errors
alias: guarantee lost; no decimal place can be proved; decimal arithmetic on parameters; DecimalRounded arithmetic
keywords: DecimalRounded, arithmetic, parameter, guarantee, proof, rounding cell, decay, from_decimal_rounded, round
related: concept.decimal-rounded stdlib.std::float64::from_decimal_rounded stdlib.std::decimal_rounded::round error.decimal-precision
docs: DECIMAL-ROUNDED-MILESTONE-1.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DECIMAL-GUARANTEE-LOST

== MEANING
Of a DecimalRounded value that is not a constant the compiler knows only
its guarantee: rounding it to N places gives the intended decimal, so the
intended number lies somewhere in that decimal's rounding cell. Rounding
is discontinuous: operands anywhere in their cells can carry an exact sum,
difference, product or quotient across a rounding boundary of every
precision, and the type states no magnitude. No decimal place of the
result can be proved, so the operation is rejected:

    no decimal place of this sum can be proved: its operands are
    DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2} and
    DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}, ...

Unary minus is exact (rounding half to even is symmetric) and keeps its
operand's type; comparisons and conversions are observations, not
arithmetic, and are allowed. Only arithmetic of decimal constants is
proven (error.decimal-precision).

== WHAT TO DO
Compute with Float64 and say where the guarantee is established again: decay
each operand with std::float64::from_decimal_rounded (the Float64 of its
guaranteed decimal), compute, and round the Float64 result back with
std::decimal_rounded::round[DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: N}](x),
which quantizes it to N places.

@example reject
fn sum(a: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}, b: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}) -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}:
    a + b

0.25.sum(0.5)
@caption The sum of two guaranteed values proves nothing
@error CORE SEMANTIC DECIMAL-GUARANTEE-LOST
@at 2:5
@end

@example accept
import std::float64
import std::decimal_rounded

fn sum(a: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}, b: DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}) -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2} errors NonFiniteFloat, UnrepresentableDecimal:
    total = std::float64::from_decimal_rounded(a) + std::float64::from_decimal_rounded(b)
    std::decimal_rounded::round[DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}](total)

0.25.sum(0.5):
    on NonFiniteFloat:
        0.00
    on UnrepresentableDecimal:
        0.00
@caption Decay, compute, round back
@value 0.75
@end

@topic error.export-signature
title: EXPORT-SIGNATURE: an exported c fn's signature has no C form
summary: An `exported c fn` declares every parameter as int, bool, Float64 or str and its result as one of those or unit; no flags, context parameters, variadic parameter or resume clause.
index: errors
alias: exported c fn parameter type; export signature; C export type
keywords: exported c fn, int64_t, double, bool, str, unit, -> unit, flags, context, variadic
related: backend.native-library error.export-environment error.export-placement error.export-name
docs: NATIVE-LIBRARY-MILESTONE-1.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-SIGNATURE

== MEANING
A C export's C ABI is read from its declaration alone -- never from how
Botlish code calls it -- so every boundary type must be declared, and must
be one the C boundary converts: int (int64_t), bool, Float64 (double), str
(text: a pointer and a length in, an owned buffer out) and, for the
result, unit. A List, a struct, an undeclared parameter or an undeclared
result has no C form.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the types (`-> unit` for no result). For a richer value, export an
adapter that converts it to a boundary type -- e.g. a table as a str -- and
keep the rich function an ordinary one.

@example reject
exported c fn total(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    0
@caption A List has no C form
@error CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-SIGNATURE
@at 1:20
@end

@example accept
exported c fn twice(n: int) -> int:
    n * 2

twice(21)
@caption Declared boundary types
@end

@topic error.export-placement
title: EXPORT-PLACEMENT: a C export is a top-level function
summary: An `exported c fn` is declared at the top level of a program or a module, never inside another function.
index: errors
alias: nested exported c fn; export inside a function
keywords: exported c fn, top level, nested function, -export
related: backend.native-library error.export-signature
docs: NATIVE-LIBRARY-MILESTONE-1.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-PLACEMENT

== MEANING
A C export is a function the library's host calls directly, with no
enclosing call: it is declared where the program's or a module's other
functions are. The same kind refuses a name -export asks for that is no
top-level function of the entry program.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the function to the top level of its file.

@example reject
fn outer() -> int:
    exported c fn inner(n: int) -> int:
        n
    inner(2)

outer()
@caption Declared inside another function
@error CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-PLACEMENT
@at 2:24
@end

@topic error.export-name
title: EXPORT-NAME: a C export's name is a C identifier
summary: An `exported c fn`'s name is the suffix of its C symbol (LIBRARY_NAME), so it is a C identifier: ASCII letters, digits and _, not starting with a digit.
index: errors
alias: exported c fn name; question mark in an export
keywords: exported c fn, C identifier, symbol, predicate, ?
related: backend.native-library error.export-signature
docs: NATIVE-LIBRARY-MILESTONE-1.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-NAME

== WHAT TO DO
Rename the export (a predicate `ready?` can be exported as `is_ready`,
calling the predicate).

@example reject
exported c fn ready?(n: int) -> bool:
    n > 0
@caption `?` is no C identifier character
@error CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-NAME
@at 1:21
@end

@topic error.export-environment
title: EXPORT-ENVIRONMENT: a C export is environment-free
summary: An `exported c fn` captures no value, requires no context and never suspends: a C caller has no environment, no installed context and no coroutine to give it.
index: errors
alias: exported c fn captures; export requires a context; export yields
keywords: exported c fn, capture, closure, context, yield, coroutine, module static
related: backend.native-library error.export-signature concept.contexts
docs: NATIVE-LIBRARY-MILESTONE-1.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-ENVIRONMENT

== MEANING
Calling other top-level functions is fine; reading a top-level value of
the entry program is capturing it. A module's top-level value is a module
static, which an export may read.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the value into an imported module, or into the function.

@example reject
limit = 3

exported c fn capped(n: int) -> int:
    if n > limit:
        return limit
    n

capped(5)
@caption Captures the entry program's `limit`
@error CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-ENVIRONMENT
@at 3:21
@end
